window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Axioms, inference rules, and proofs", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Axioms, inference rules, and proofs § A proof starts from accepted assumptions and reaches a conclusion through allowed steps. The starting assumptions are called axioms . The allowed steps are specified by rules of inference . For example, if we know \\(P\\) and know that \\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\), we may conclude \\(Q\\). In the even-integer example from the introduction, each step used a definition or an arithmetic rule. If we record these justifications explicitly, another person can check the argument without having to discover it again. Now suppose the statements and rules are written precisely enough for each step to be checked by following fixed instructions. A machine could then check a supplied proof. This is the connection we need: reasoning can be represented as a finite sequence of operations. Each arrow shows a dependency between proof steps. The same inference rule first gives \\(Q\\) from \\(P\\), then \\(R\\) from \\(Q\\). Problems From the axioms \\(P\\), “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” and “\\(Q\\) implies \\(R\\),” with modus ponens as the only rule of inference, write a proof of \\(R\\). Give the justification of each line. Prove that the sum of an even integer and an odd integer is odd, giving the justification for each step. A checker accepts every line of a proof. Show by an example that the conclusion can still be false, and say exactly what the accepted proof establishes."}, {"title": "Hilbert’s program and consistency", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Hilbert’s program and consistency § Writing rules explicitly lets us check whether an argument follows them. But are the rules themselves reliable? Could they allow us to prove both a statement and its opposite? A system that never does this is called consistent . Hilbert wanted to put mathematics on a secure foundation. First, its assumptions and proof rules would be made explicit. Then one would show that those rules could never produce a contradiction. He hoped to give this justification using reasoning about finite objects and concrete operations on them. These were called finitary methods . Even when a mathematical statement concerns infinitely many numbers, its written proof is finite. So, proofs and the rules for constructing them could themselves be studied. This was a question about the reliability of mathematical reasoning. A related question concerns its automation: once the rules are explicit, can a procedure always determine whether a statement has a proof? To see the difficulty, we must distinguish checking a proof from searching for one. Hilbert’s aim was to study the finite proofs allowed by a system and show that none could lead to a contradiction. Problems A system has the axioms \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies not \\(P\\),” and the rule modus ponens. Is it consistent? Suppose a system also has the rule “from \\(A\\) and not \\(A\\), infer any statement \\(B\\).” Show that if the system is inconsistent, it proves every statement. Hilbert hoped to prove that systems are consistent. Show that a consistency proof carried out inside the same system \\(S\\) could not settle the doubt, by showing that an inconsistent \\(S\\) of the kind in the previous problem proves the statement “\\(S\\) is consistent.”"}, {"title": "Proof checking, proof search, and decision", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-3", "text": "1.1.3 Proof checking, proof search, and decision § Suppose we are given a proposed proof. We can check its steps and accept or reject that particular argument. Rejection does not show that the conclusion has no proof; a different argument might work. What if no proof is supplied? Suppose proofs use a fixed, finite collection of symbols. We can list candidate strings in order of length and check each one. Most will not be proofs. But every finite proof eventually appears, so this search finds a proof whenever one exists. What happens if there is no proof? The search keeps trying longer candidates. It never reaches a last candidate that certifies failure. A decision procedure must do more. For every statement we give it, it must finish with a correct answer: “a proof exists” or “no proof exists.” Our search method does not meet that requirement. Could another method succeed? Failure of one search strategy does not show that every possible strategy fails. To investigate that question, we need to specify what counts as a mechanical procedure. This leads us to models of computation . Checking tests one proposed proof. Searching tries candidates until it finds a proof. Deciding must also finish when no proof exists. Problems Strings over the alphabet \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) are listed by length, starting with the empty string, and alphabetically within each length. Write the first seven strings, and give the position of \\(ab\\). A proof search lists candidate strings over a two-symbol alphabet in this order and checks each one. If the shortest proof of a statement has length \\(5\\), at most how many candidates are checked before a proof is found? Why can such a bound not be computed in advance for an arbitrary statement? Suppose a theory is consistent, its axioms can be listed by an algorithm, and it is complete: for every sentence \\(P\\), it proves \\(P\\) or it proves not \\(P\\). Show that searching for a proof of \\(P\\) and a proof of not \\(P\\) in alternation is a decision procedure for provability."}, {"title": "Algorithms and termination", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Algorithms and termination § Let us first consider a procedure whose steps we can examine directly. We want the greatest common divisor of two positive integers: the largest positive integer that divides both without remainder. Euclid’s algorithm repeatedly divides and keeps the remainder. For \\(48\\) and \\(18\\), we obtain \\[48=2\\cdot18+12,\\qquad18=1\\cdot12+6,\\qquad12=2\\cdot6+0.\\] The pairs change as follows: \\[(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12)\\longrightarrow(12,6)\\longrightarrow(6,0).\\] At a step with pair \\((a,b)\\), suppose \\(b\\) is positive. Divide \\(a\\) by \\(b\\). Write the result as \\(a=qb+r\\), where \\(q\\) is the quotient and \\(r\\) is the remainder. The remainder satisfies \\(0\\le r\\lt b\\). Replace \\((a,b)\\) by \\((b,r)\\) and repeat. When the second entry becomes zero, return the first. In our example, we reach \\((6,0)\\), so the answer is six. Why does this give the correct answer? Suppose a number divides both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). It must also divide \\(a-qb\\), which is the remainder \\(r\\). So, every common divisor of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is a common divisor of \\(b\\) and \\(r\\). The reverse also holds. A number dividing both \\(b\\) and \\(r\\) divides \\(qb+r=a\\). Thus the old pair and the new pair have exactly the same common divisors. In particular, their greatest common divisor is unchanged. A property that remains unchanged at every step is called an invariant . Why does the procedure finish? Each new second entry is smaller than the previous one, and it is never negative. A sequence of nonnegative integers cannot keep decreasing forever. Eventually the second entry is zero. At that point the pair is \\((g,0)\\). Its greatest common divisor is \\(g\\): every divisor of \\(g\\) also divides zero. Since the greatest common divisor has stayed unchanged, \\(g\\) is the answer for the original pair. We have established two things: the steps preserve the answer, and the process reaches its stopping condition. These require separate arguments. A procedure that repeats the same pair forever preserves the greatest common divisor but never returns it. So, preserving the answer alone is not enough. The greatest common divisor remains six throughout this run. The second entry decreases through nonnegative integers until it is zero. These are separate reasons for correctness and termination. Problems Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((91,35)\\), writing each division and each pair. Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((21,13)\\). How many divisions does it take, and why does this pair make the second entry decrease slowly? Give a procedure on pairs of positive integers that keeps the greatest common divisor unchanged at every step but never stops, and another that always stops but can return a wrong answer. What does this show about the two arguments for Euclid’s algorithm?"}, {"title": "Configurations and transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Configurations and transitions § Suppose we pause Euclid’s algorithm at the pair \\((18,12)\\), just before the next division. What must we record in order to continue? We need the pair and the instruction that comes next. We do not need the earlier pairs. The information needed to resume a computation is called its configuration . What must we include? That depends on where we allow the computation to pause. At the start of Euclid’s repeated step, we need only the pair: the next instruction is already fixed. Halfway through a division, the pair alone would not be enough. We would also need the temporary values and a record of how far the division had progressed. A transition is one step from a configuration to the next. Starting with a particular input, we obtain a sequence of configurations. This sequence is called a run . The algorithm supplies the transition rules and tells us when to stop. Proof search can be described in the same way. We record the candidate being checked, the line currently under examination, and any other information needed by the checker. Each operation updates this configuration. For a deterministic procedure, the current configuration determines the next step. Does it also determine the previous step? In Euclid’s algorithm, both \\((30,18)\\) and \\((48,18)\\) lead to \\((18,12)\\). So, knowing how to continue does not necessarily tell us how to go backward. We will return to this distinction when we consider reversibility. A configuration includes enough information to resume the computation. A transition updates that information; a run is a sequence of these updates. Problems Euclid’s algorithm divides by repeated subtraction. It is paused in the middle of dividing \\(48\\) by \\(18\\), after one subtraction has given \\(30\\). What must the configuration record so that the computation can resume? Write the run of Euclid’s algorithm from \\((30,18)\\) as a sequence of configurations. Find two other pairs whose runs reach \\((18,12)\\) after one step. Describe all pairs from which Euclid’s algorithm reaches \\((18,12)\\) in one step. What does the answer imply about running the algorithm backward?"}, {"title": "Turing machines and universal computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-3", "text": "1.2.3 Turing machines and universal computation § We have described computation as a sequence of transitions. But an instruction such as “divide” still contains many operations. To study computation in general, we need a model with explicitly specified elementary steps. A Turing machine has a tape divided into cells. Each cell holds a symbol from a fixed, finite collection, including a blank symbol. A head reads one cell at a time. The machine also has an internal state chosen from a finite list. The state records which stage of its instructions it is in. A rule table uses two pieces of information: the current state and the symbol under the head. For each relevant pair, the rule tells the machine what to write, whether to move one cell left or right, and which state to enter next. If it enters the halting state, the computation stops. For example, place a finite block of \\(1\\) symbols on an otherwise blank tape, with the head at the left end. Give the machine the rule to move right whenever it reads \\(1\\), and to halt when it reads a blank. The same finite instructions work for a block of any finite length. To resume this machine, we need the tape contents, the head position, and the internal state. Together, these make up its configuration. The model does not fix a maximum tape length in advance. More cells are available when needed. This does not mean that a completed calculation uses infinite memory: after finitely many steps, the head has visited only finitely many cells. With more rules, the machine can carry out arithmetic or check proofs. Its own rule table is also a finite object, so we can encode that table as a string of symbols. Another machine can read this description and imitate the described machine, one step at a time. A universal Turing machine can do this for any correctly encoded machine and input. Changing the description changes the computation it simulates. Now a program can be treated as data. We can supply its description to another program, or even supply it to itself. This requires only copying a finite string. It will be useful when we ask what programs can determine about other programs. This gives a general model of computation. We can use it to describe particular algorithms and to formulate questions about all machines in the model. The highlighted cell is under the head. In this example, the machine moves right over each 1 and halts at a blank. A universal machine uses encoded instructions to simulate another machine; it need not halt if the simulated run does not halt. Problems A machine has a running state \\(q\\) and a halting state \\(h\\). In \\(q\\), reading \\(1\\), it writes \\(1\\), moves right, and stays in \\(q\\); reading a blank, it halts. Started on the leftmost \\(1\\) of a tape holding \\(111\\), list its configurations until it halts. Change the machine so that, reading a blank in state \\(q\\), it writes \\(1\\) and halts. What does the new machine compute on a block of \\(n\\) ones, and how many steps does it take? Design a machine that, started on the leftmost symbol of a block of ones, halts in a state marked “even” if the block has an even number of ones and “odd” otherwise. Give its rule table and trace it on \\(111\\)."}, {"title": "The Church–Turing thesis", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 The Church–Turing thesis § We have defined a model using a tape, a head, and a finite rule table. Why use this model to discuss the limits of effective calculation? Could another reasonable model compute functions that Turing machines cannot? Other mathematical models describe computation in different ways. Lambda calculus uses rules for substituting expressions. Recursive-function models build functions from specified basic functions and construction rules. Despite these differences, they compute the same functions as Turing machines. A function computable in one model is computable in the others. This equivalence is a mathematical result that can be proved. The Church–Turing thesis makes a further claim: any calculation that can be carried out by an effective mechanical method can be carried out by a Turing machine. The phrase “effective mechanical method” is an informal idea. A Turing machine is a precisely defined object. The thesis connects the two. The agreement between different formal models supports it, but does not turn it into a theorem about an already defined class of every possible mechanical method. This distinction matters when we establish a limitation. We can prove that no Turing machine solves a specified problem. Applying that result to effective procedures in general uses the thesis. The thesis concerns whether a function can be computed at all. It does not say how much time or memory the computation needs. This matters for quantum computing. In the standard quantum model, the allowed operations must themselves have effective mathematical descriptions. Under this condition, quantum computers do not compute functions that are uncomputable by Turing machines. They can change the resources needed for some tasks that are already computable. We will return to this difference when studying quantum algorithms. The solid arrows connect formal models that can simulate one another. They compute the same functions, though they may take different amounts of time. The dashed arrow marks the further claim of the Church–Turing thesis: these models capture what we mean informally by an effective method. Problems A programming language \\(L\\) can simulate every Turing machine, and a Turing machine can simulate every program of \\(L\\). Which functions does \\(L\\) compute? Is this conclusion a theorem or an instance of the Church–Turing thesis? Classify each statement as a theorem or as the Church–Turing thesis: (a) lambda calculus and Turing machines compute the same functions; (b) every function computable by an effective mechanical method is Turing-computable; (c) every recursive function is Turing-computable. Show that some functions from nonnegative integers to \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) are computed by no Turing machine. Turing machines can be listed as \\(M_0,M_1,M_2,\\dots\\), since each has a finite description."}, {"title": "The halting problem", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 The halting problem § Given a program and its input, can we always determine whether the computation will stop? We know that Euclid’s algorithm stops for its permitted inputs. We also know that a program instructed to repeat forever does not stop. Can one procedure settle this question for every program and input? Suppose such a procedure exists. Call it \\(H(P,x)\\), where \\(P\\) is a program description and \\(x\\) is its input. We require \\(H\\) itself to finish and answer correctly on every valid pair. Let us use \\(H\\) to construct another program, \\(D\\). Its input \\(p\\) is a program description. First, \\(D\\) calls \\(H(p,p)\\): it asks what happens when the program described by \\(p\\) receives its own description as input. Then \\(D\\) takes the opposite action. If \\(H\\) answers “halts,” \\(D\\) enters an endless loop. If \\(H\\) answers “does not halt,” \\(D\\) stops. If the input is not a valid program description, we can simply let \\(D\\) stop. Now let \\(d\\) be the description of \\(D\\), and give \\(d\\) to \\(D\\). What should \\(H(d,d)\\) answer? If it answers “halts,” the instructions of \\(D\\) make this run continue forever. If it answers “does not halt,” the instructions make the run stop. In either case, the prediction is wrong. We assumed that \\(H\\) was an algorithm that always finished and answered correctly. That assumption allowed us to use it as part of \\(D\\). But the resulting program contradicts the assumption. Therefore no such general \\(H\\) exists. This result is called the undecidability of the halting problem . This does not prevent us from determining the behavior of particular programs. We already proved that Euclid’s algorithm terminates. It also permits an analyzer that sometimes answers “unknown.” What is excluded is one algorithm that always finishes with a correct definite answer for every case. Assume \\(H\\) always finishes and predicts correctly. Program \\(D\\) follows its prediction and takes the opposite action. Now give \\(D\\) its own description \\(d\\). \\(H(d,d)\\) is predicting this very run, so either answer is contradicted by what \\(D\\) does. Problems Decide, by inspecting each program, whether it halts on the input \\(x=5\\): (a) while \\(x\\gt 0\\), set \\(x:=x-1\\); (b) while \\(x\\neq0\\), set \\(x:=x-2\\); (c) repeat forever: do nothing. The program “while \\(x\\neq1\\): if \\(x\\) is even, set \\(x:=x/2\\), otherwise \\(x:=3x+1\\)” is run on \\(6\\) and on \\(7\\). How many steps does each run take? Why do these successful runs not give a halting decider for this program? Show that no algorithm decides, for every program \\(P\\), whether \\(P\\) halts when given the input \\(0\\). Use the undecidability of the halting problem: from a pair \\((P,x)\\), construct a program that ignores its own input."}, {"title": "Undecidability and incompleteness", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-3", "text": "1.3.3 Undecidability and incompleteness § We began by asking whether explicit rules could make mathematical reasoning reliable and whether a procedure could settle every question about proofs. The halting result shows how to prove a limitation on procedures. We now briefly consider the corresponding limits on what a system of axioms can prove. A problem is undecidable if no algorithm always finishes with the correct yes-or-no answer for every input. A theory is incomplete if some sentence can neither be proved nor refuted using its axioms and rules. The first is a limitation on procedures; the second is a limitation on what follows from chosen assumptions. To state the Gödel–Rosser incompleteness theorem, first consider a theory with three properties. It is consistent. Its axioms can be listed by an algorithm. And it contains enough arithmetic to express basic calculations with nonnegative integers. For a concrete example of the last requirement, take Peano arithmetic. This is the usual first-order theory of nonnegative integers with addition, multiplication, and induction. The theorem applies to consistent theories that include these axioms and whose axioms can be listed algorithmically. It also applies to certain weaker arithmetic theories. The conclusion is that some sentence \\(P\\) has neither a proof nor a refutation in the theory. The theory proves neither \\(P\\) nor \\(\\neg P\\). A theory whose axioms can be listed by an algorithm is called effectively axiomatized . The list may be infinite: each axiom only needs to appear after finitely many steps. A proof can include a record showing when a cited axiom appears on that list. This lets a checker verify the use of that axiom in finite time. Can we settle an unanswered sentence by adding it as a new axiom? We can. But if the enlarged theory still meets the theorem’s conditions, some sentence will again remain unsettled. It need not be the same sentence. The result does not apply to every formal theory; the conditions matter. Gödel’s second incompleteness theorem concerns the consistency question. A theory with enough arithmetic can write statements about its own encoded proofs. In particular, it can express the claim that none of those proofs derives a contradiction. For theories satisfying the theorem’s arithmetic and proof-representation conditions, the result is this: if the theory is consistent, it cannot prove that standard statement of its own consistency. So, a consistency argument whose steps can all be carried out within the same theory cannot provide this justification. A stronger theory may prove that a weaker one is consistent. But we must then examine the assumptions of the stronger theory. So, formal reasoning has precise limitations. We can still prove particular statements and construct algorithms for particular tasks. For the tasks that admit algorithms, we can now ask how those algorithms should be implemented. On the left, we ask whether an algorithm can decide a problem for every input. On the right, we ask what a particular theory can prove. The incompleteness result requires the stated conditions on that theory. An unsuccessful search alone establishes neither result. Problems A theory has the single axiom \\(P\\) and the rule modus ponens, and its sentences are built from \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\). Is \\(Q\\) provable? Is not \\(Q\\) provable? Is the theory complete? A sentence \\(G\\) is neither provable nor refutable in a consistent theory \\(T\\). Show that both \\(T\\) with \\(G\\) added as an axiom and \\(T\\) with not \\(G\\) added are consistent. You may use the fact that if \\(T\\) with an added axiom \\(A\\) proves a contradiction, then \\(T\\) proves not \\(A\\). Suppose an algorithm extends a consistent, effectively axiomatized theory of arithmetic step by step, each time adding a sentence that the current theory leaves undecided. Show that the combined theory, with all the added axioms together, is still incomplete if it is consistent."}, {"title": "Deterministic and reversible transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Deterministic and reversible transitions § In a deterministic computation, each configuration has at most one next configuration. A halting configuration may have none. When the computation can continue, the current configuration fixes what happens next. Does knowing the later configuration also tell us which earlier one produced it? Determinism does not require this. For example, Euclid’s algorithm includes the transitions \\[(30,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12),\\qquad(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12).\\] Knowing the pair \\((18,12)\\) lets us continue the calculation, but it does not identify which earlier pair occurred. Different configurations have merged into the same one. To undo a step, we must be able to recover the earlier configuration from the full resulting configuration. This requires different inputs to have different outputs. Such a transition is called one-to-one . Logical reversibility requires this extra condition. Suppose the transition also reaches every configuration in a fixed state space. It is then a bijection : each configuration has exactly one predecessor. We can define the reverse transformation everywhere on that space. So, knowing what happens next does not necessarily tell us what happened before. We now ask whether useful computations can preserve enough information to be reversed. On the left, each input has a unique successor, but \\(C\\) has two possible predecessors. On the right, each output identifies one predecessor. Reversibility imposes a condition on the backward direction as well. Problems A rule on the configurations \\(A,B,C,D\\) sends \\(A\\to B\\), \\(B\\to C\\), \\(C\\to D\\), and \\(D\\to B\\). Is it deterministic? Which configurations have more than one predecessor, and which have none? Run the same rule for six steps from each of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\). Which starting configurations end at the same configuration after six steps? Prove that a deterministic, one-to-one rule on finitely many configurations is onto, and that every run of such a rule eventually returns to its starting configuration."}, {"title": "Information loss in computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 Information loss in computation § Consider the Boolean AND operation. Its output is one only when both input bits are one. The inputs \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) all produce zero. If we retain only this output, we cannot recover the input pair. Must we discard the input to obtain the answer? Suppose we want to calculate \\(f(x)\\). If we replace \\(x\\) by its answer, two different inputs may become indistinguishable. If we keep both the input and the answer as \\((x,f(x))\\), the input remains available. This suggests using extra storage while carrying out the calculation. Keeping the input is only a starting idea. We must choose elementary steps that can be undone. We must say what values the extra storage holds at the start. We must also decide what to do with temporary results. The next chapter develops these constructions using Boolean functions and circuits. Why does this matter for quantum computing? When a quantum system evolves without interacting with anything outside it, its evolution is described by a unitary transformation . Such a transformation has an inverse: mathematically, it can be undone. This is why reversible operations are important in quantum circuits. We need to learn how to obtain an answer through steps that preserve this possibility of reversal. Measurement and discarding part of a system are different kinds of operations, which we will treat separately. We have moved from asking whether a task admits an algorithm to asking how the algorithm transforms information. The limits of computability do not settle this implementation question. We now need to examine the operations used in the computation itself. On the left, the AND output does not tell us which input produced it. On the right, we keep both input bits and put their AND in an extra target bit, initially zero. The full update uses XOR, or addition modulo two. Applying it twice restores the original three bits. Chapter 2 explains the construction. Problems Which of these operations on two bits lose information: \\((x,y)\\mapsto x\\land y\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,x\\oplus y)\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto(y,x)\\); \\((x,y)\\mapsto x\\oplus y\\)? Keep both inputs and store the AND in an extra bit \\(t\\) that starts at \\(0\\): \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\land y))\\). List the outputs for the four inputs with \\(t=0\\), and show that the map can be undone. A computation maps \\(n\\)-bit inputs to a single output bit. Show that at least \\(2^{n-1}\\) inputs share an output. If the computation keeps some bits besides its output, how many must it keep, at least, to become one-to-one, and how many always suffice?"}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-1", "text": "A proof search over a three-symbol alphabet has checked every candidate of length at most \\(10\\), including the empty string, without finding a proof. How many candidates is that, and what does the failure establish? \\(1+3+\\dots+3^{10}=(3^{11}-1)/2=88{,}573\\) candidates. The failure shows only that the statement has no proof of length at most \\(10\\). A longer proof may exist, and no finite search can establish that none does."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run Euclid’s algorithm on \\((377,233)\\). Count the divisions, record each quotient, and explain separately why the answer is correct and why the run stops. The remainders are \\(144,89,55,34,21,13,8,5,3,2,1,0\\): twelve divisions, with quotient \\(1\\) every time except the last, which is \\(2\\). The greatest common divisor is \\(1\\). It is correct because each step keeps the set of common divisors unchanged, an invariant. The run stops because the second entry strictly decreases through nonnegative integers. Quotients of \\(1\\) make that decrease as slow as possible."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-3", "text": "Make each step of Euclid’s algorithm reversible by recording its quotient. Show that \\((b,r)\\) and \\(q\\) determine the previous pair, and find how many quotients a run on \\((377,233)\\) must keep. From \\((b,r)\\) and \\(q\\), the previous first entry is \\(a=qb+r\\), so the previous pair \\((a,b)\\) is recovered. A run that keeps all its quotients can be undone step by step. The run on \\((377,233)\\) keeps twelve quotients, eleven \\(1\\)s and one \\(2\\). Reversibility costs extra memory that grows with the length of the run."}, {"title": "Binary encodings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Binary encodings § A computation acts on configurations made of bits. How, then, can it work with numbers, text, or graphs? We choose an encoding : a rule that writes each object as a string of bits. An integer can be written in binary, text character by character, and a graph as a list of its edges. With two bits, for example, the integers \\(0,1,2,3\\) become \\(00,01,10,11\\). The bits themselves carry no meaning. The string \\(01000001\\) is the integer \\(65\\) in binary and the letter A in the ASCII character code. Meaning belongs to the encoding. Why does the choice of encoding matter? It fixes the length \\(n\\) of the input, and the cost of a computation will be measured against \\(n\\). With \\(n\\) bits there are \\(2^n\\) possible inputs. For a fixed length \\(n\\), a computation is then a function from \\(n\\)-bit strings to bit strings. A procedure that works for every length is a family of such functions, one for each \\(n\\), built in a systematic way rather than separately for each length. An encoding supplies meaning. The bits alone do not tell a machine whether to treat \\(01000001\\) as a number or a character. Problems Write \\(13\\) in binary with four bits, and decode \\(1011\\) as an unsigned binary integer. An integer \\(N\\) is written in binary and, alternatively, in unary, as \\(N\\) marks. For \\(N=1000\\), compare the two input lengths. How does a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps compare with each length? Show that any encoding of the integers \\(0,1,\\dots,M-1\\) by bit strings of one fixed length \\(n\\) needs \\(n\\geq\\log_2M\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean functions and truth tables", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 Boolean functions and truth tables § What exactly do we want to compute? Take a yes/no question about an \\(n\\)-bit input. Its answer is a Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), which assigns \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) to every input. An answer of several bits is a list of such functions, one for each output bit. The most direct way to specify \\(f\\) is to list every input together with its output. This list is the truth table of \\(f\\). For AND and OR on two bits: Input \\(xy\\) AND OR \\(00\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) \\(01\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(10\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(11\\) \\(1\\) \\(1\\) Does a truth table tell us how to compute \\(f\\)? It tells us what the answer is, not how to obtain it. It also grows quickly: a function of \\(n\\) bits has \\(2^n\\) rows, so the table alone is exponentially large. We want a description built from a few simple operations. The truth table lists four possible inputs. Only the input \\(11\\) produces one; a circuit must implement all four rows. Problems Write the truth table of the majority function on three bits, which outputs \\(1\\) when at least two of its inputs are \\(1\\). How many Boolean functions of two bits are there? List those that output \\(1\\) on exactly one input. Show that there are \\(2^{2^n}\\) Boolean functions of \\(n\\) bits. For \\(n=10\\), show that fewer than one function in a thousand can be described by a string of fewer than \\(1014\\) bits."}, {"title": "Polynomial representation over the binary field", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Polynomial representation over the binary field § Can a truth table be turned into a formula? Use arithmetic modulo two, in which \\(1+1=0\\). Addition modulo two is then XOR, and multiplication is AND. The idea is to build, for each row \\(s\\) of the table, a detector that equals \\(1\\) on the input \\(s\\) and \\(0\\) on every other input. The factor \\(x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=1\\), and the factor \\(1+x_i\\) equals \\(1\\) exactly when \\(x_i=0\\). Multiplying one such factor for each bit gives \\[\\delta_s(x)=\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=1}x_i\\prod_{i:\\,s_i=0}(1+x_i).\\] For two bits \\((x,y)\\), the detector of the row \\(01\\) is \\((1+x)y\\). Now add the detectors of the rows on which \\(f\\) equals \\(1\\): \\[f(x)=\\sum_{s:\\,f(s)=1}\\delta_s(x)\\pmod 2.\\] Why is this \\(f\\)? On any input \\(x\\), exactly one detector, \\(\\delta_x\\), equals \\(1\\). If the row \\(x\\) is among those added, the sum is \\(1\\); otherwise it is \\(0\\). For OR, the rows with output \\(1\\) are \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\): \\[(1+x)y+x(1+y)+xy=x+y+3xy=x+y+xy\\pmod 2.\\] Check the row \\(11\\): \\(1+1+1=1\\) modulo two, as required, whereas ordinary addition would give \\(3\\). Expanding the detectors in this way writes every Boolean function as a polynomial in its input bits. All additions are modulo two. The polynomial \\(x+y+xy\\) reproduces OR on Boolean inputs; its interpretation depends on this arithmetic. Problems Evaluate \\((1+x)(1+y)\\) modulo two for all four inputs. Which function is it? Build the polynomial of XOR from its truth table using detectors, and simplify it. Show that the polynomial of the three-bit majority function is \\(xy+yz+xz\\) modulo two."}, {"title": "Polynomial evaluation by circuits", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Polynomial evaluation by circuits § How do we evaluate \\(x+y+xy\\) one operation at a time? Compute \\(u=xy\\) with an AND gate, compute \\(x+y\\) with an XOR gate, and combine the two results with a second XOR gate. This arrangement is a circuit . Its gates are elementary operations, and its wires carry bits from one gate to the next; here a wire carries \\(u\\) from the AND gate to the last XOR gate. A wire may also carry a fixed bit, \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). No wire leads back to an earlier gate, so the gates can be evaluated in order. The same method works for every polynomial: multiply factors with AND gates and add terms with XOR gates. So every Boolean function of a fixed number of bits has a circuit. Does it have a small circuit? Not necessarily: the polynomial built from a truth table can have exponentially many terms. That every function can be built is one claim; that it can be built economically is another. To evaluate \\(z=ab+c\\) over bits, compute the product once and pass that bit to XOR. Here \\(+\\) means XOR, so the diagram is also a circuit plan. Problems Evaluate the circuit for \\(x+y+xy\\), built from one AND gate and two XOR gates, on the input \\(10\\), giving the bit on every wire. Build a circuit for majority, \\(xy+yz+xz\\), from AND and XOR gates. How many gates does it use? The parity function \\(x_1+x_2+\\dots+x_n\\pmod2\\) equals \\(1\\) on half of its inputs. How many detectors does the truth-table construction add, and how many XOR gates does the simplified polynomial need?"}, {"title": "NAND universality", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 NAND universality § Do we need AND, XOR, and NOT as separate kinds of gate? One kind is enough. NAND outputs the negation of AND, \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)=\\neg(a\\land b)\\); it is \\(0\\) only when \\(a=b=1\\). Tying both inputs to the same bit gives NOT: \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,a)=\\neg a\\). Negating a NAND output gives AND. XOR takes four NAND gates: \\[t=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\quad u=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,t),\\quad v=\\operatorname{NAND}(b,t),\\quad a\\oplus b=\\operatorname{NAND}(u,v).\\] Checking the four input pairs confirms the last identity. Since NAND builds AND, XOR, and NOT, it can evaluate the polynomial of any Boolean function. A gate with this property is universal . In this construction one bit often feeds several gates: \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) each feed two NAND gates, and so does \\(t\\). Copying a bit in this way, called fan-out , is free in classical circuits. Four NAND gates compute XOR. Branch dots indicate shared classical inputs; small output circles indicate negation. Problems Compute \\(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\) for all four inputs. Use the table to check that \\(\\operatorname{NAND}\\bigl(\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b)\\bigr)=a\\land b\\). Build OR from NAND gates, and verify the construction on all four inputs. Show that circuits of AND and XOR gates, with no constant inputs, cannot compute NOT, but that adding the constant \\(1\\) makes the set universal."}, {"title": "Irreversible Boolean operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 Irreversible Boolean operations § Given the output of an AND gate, can we recover its inputs? If the output is \\(1\\), yes: the input was \\(11\\). If it is \\(0\\), the input could have been \\(00\\), \\(01\\), or \\(10\\). Three inputs merge into one output, so the map has no inverse. This is logical information loss , and it happens whether or not the gate computes something useful. Is a circuit irreversible whenever one of its gates is? Not in its overall effect: a circuit can compute a one-to-one function of its input while erasing intermediate values along the way. Its truth table is then reversible , but its individual steps are not. Whether information is lost is a question about each step, not only about the overall truth table. The AND gate maps \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) to the same output. Output zero cannot identify which of these inputs occurred. Problems For OR on two bits, list the inputs that share each output. Can the inputs be recovered from the output? A circuit computes \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x\\land y,\\;x\\lor y)\\). Is this map one-to-one? Which inputs collide? The map \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,\\,x\\oplus y)\\) is computed by copying \\(x\\) and feeding \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) into an XOR gate, which on its own loses information. Show that the map is one-to-one, and explain where the information discarded by the XOR gate survives."}, {"title": "Landauer’s principle", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Landauer’s principle § Does losing information cost anything physically? A bit is stored in a physical system, such as a capacitor that is charged or uncharged. Resetting an unknown bit to \\(0\\) merges two physical possibilities into one. Landauer’s principle states that erasing a bit that is equally likely to be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), at temperature \\(T\\) and without keeping a record of its value elsewhere, releases on average at least \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) of heat into the surroundings. Here \\(k_{\\mathrm B}\\) is Boltzmann’s constant. The conditions matter. A bit already known to be \\(0\\) can be left alone at no cost. A biased bit, or a bit whose value is recorded elsewhere, carries less uncertainty and needs less. The principle is not a fixed charge on every gate; it is a lower bound on the cost of destroying information. The information lost by AND, or by a reset, therefore has a physical counterpart: heat. Further detail: Bennett: Landauer’s principle and reversible computation . Both possible logical inputs are reset to zero. For erasure of one equally likely bit in the standard isothermal setting, the minimum heat delivered to the environment is kBT ln 2. Problems Compute \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) at \\(T=300\\) K, with \\(k_{\\mathrm B}=1.38\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K. A processor erases \\(10^{18}\\) bits per second at \\(300\\) K. What minimum power does Landauer’s principle require? Compare it with a processor that uses \\(50\\) W. Erasing a bit that is \\(0\\) with probability \\(p\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1-p\\) has the minimum cost \\(H\\,k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\), where \\(H=-p\\log_2p-(1-p)\\log_2(1-p)\\). Compute \\(H\\) for \\(p=0.9\\), and compare the cost with that of an unbiased bit and a known bit."}, {"title": "Logical reversibility and dissipation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Logical reversibility and dissipation § If a computation never erases information, does it produce no heat? Not necessarily. A logically reversible map, one that is one-to-one on its allowed states, avoids the cost that erasure forces. A real device may still dissipate energy through resistance, friction, imperfect control, or the speed at which it runs. Conversely, can a gate that loses information be made reversible by operating it slowly? No. Running AND slowly does not make its output reveal its input. Reversibility is a property of the map, not of how the map is carried out. Two questions must therefore be kept apart: whether the mathematical map can be inverted, and how much energy its physical realization uses. For quantum computation the first question is decisive, because the operations of an isolated quantum system can always be inverted. A reversible truth table settles whether the input is recoverable. It does not specify the energy dissipated by a particular physical device. Problems Which of these are logically reversible: NOT; AND; \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x,x\\oplus y)\\); a reset to \\(0\\)? A device implements NOT but dissipates \\(10^{-18}\\) J per operation, while the Landauer bound at \\(300\\) K is about \\(2.9\\times10^{-21}\\) J. Does the dissipation contradict the reversibility of NOT? Does Landauer’s principle require any dissipation for NOT? Show that a reversible map on \\(n\\)-bit strings is a permutation of the \\(2^n\\) strings. For \\(n=2\\), count the reversible maps and compare with the number of all maps from two bits to two bits."}, {"title": "Reversible function embeddings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-3", "text": "2.3.3 Reversible function embeddings § Can every function be computed without erasing information, even one such as AND that merges its inputs? It can, provided we keep the input. Add a target bit \\(t\\) and replace \\(f\\) by the map \\[U_f:(x,t)\\mapsto(x,\\,t\\oplus f(x)).\\] The input \\(x\\) is kept, and \\(f(x)\\) is added to the target modulo two. With \\(t=0\\), the target becomes the answer \\(f(x)\\). Why is this map reversible? Apply it twice: \\(t\\oplus f(x)\\oplus f(x)=t\\), because adding the same bit twice modulo two gives zero. The map is its own inverse. For AND it is \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\): \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\((x,y,t)\\) \\((x,y,t\\oplus xy)\\) \\(000\\) \\(000\\) \\(001\\) \\(001\\) \\(010\\) \\(010\\) \\(011\\) \\(011\\) \\(100\\) \\(100\\) \\(101\\) \\(101\\) \\(110\\) \\(111\\) \\(111\\) \\(110\\) Has AND become invertible? No: \\(f\\) itself is unchanged and still merges its inputs. What is invertible is the larger map, whose output keeps \\(x\\). The extra bits are not mere bookkeeping; they are what makes the transformation one-to-one. For \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips. Applying the same reversible oracle twice restores the input and target because XOR by the same bit cancels. Problems Write the outputs of \\(U_f:(x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\lor y))\\) for the four inputs with \\(t=0\\). Apply \\(U_f\\), with \\(f\\) the OR function, twice to \\((1,0,1)\\), showing each step. For a function \\(f\\) from \\(n\\)-bit strings to \\(m\\)-bit strings, define \\(U_f(x,t)=(x,t\\oplus f(x))\\), where \\(t\\) has \\(m\\) bits and \\(\\oplus\\) acts bit by bit. Prove that \\(U_f\\) is its own inverse for every \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Controlled reversible operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Controlled reversible operations § How can a gate act on one bit depending on another without losing anything? Keep the deciding bit unchanged, and let it choose a reversible update of the other bit. The kept bit is the control ; the updated bit is the target . The simplest example is controlled-NOT, or CNOT : \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a,b\\oplus a)\\). When the control \\(a\\) is \\(0\\), the target is unchanged; when \\(a\\) is \\(1\\), the target flips. So \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). Applying CNOT twice restores both bits. Why does this work? The control survives the gate, so we know which update was applied, and each update, doing nothing or flipping, is its own inverse. Would it be enough to make the target update reversible but discard the control? No: without the control we would not know whether to flip the target back. CNOT preserves its control bit. For control zero it leaves the target alone; for control one it flips the target. Each branch can be undone. Problems Apply CNOT with the second bit as control and the first as target to \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\). Apply three CNOT gates in turn to \\((a,b)\\): control the first bit and target the second, then control the second and target the first, then control the first and target the second. Show that the result is \\((b,a)\\). A controlled operation keeps a control bit \\(c\\) and applies a map \\(g_c\\) to the target. Prove that it is reversible when \\(g_0\\) and \\(g_1\\) are both reversible, and give its inverse. Show by an example that it is not reversible if \\(g_1\\) is a reset."}, {"title": "CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates § Is CNOT enough to build every reversible computation? Each bit produced by CNOT and NOT gates is a sum modulo two of input bits, possibly plus \\(1\\). Such gates can therefore never produce the product \\(xy\\). The Toffoli gate adds a second control: \\((a,b,c)\\mapsto(a,b,c\\oplus ab)\\). With its target starting at \\(0\\), it writes the AND of its controls while keeping them. With a control fixed at \\(1\\) it acts as CNOT, and with both controls fixed at \\(1\\) as NOT. Since AND and NOT suffice for every Boolean function, Toffoli gates, together with extra bits set to fixed values, can compute every Boolean function reversibly. Is the Toffoli gate the only choice? The Fredkin gate keeps one control and swaps two targets when the control is \\(1\\). With the second target fixed at \\(0\\), it sends \\((a,b,0)\\) to \\((a,\\,b\\land\\neg a,\\,ab)\\): it too writes the AND, and so it too computes every Boolean function reversibly. Toffoli and Fredkin gates are each their own inverse. In circuit drawings, a filled dot marks a control that acts when it is \\(1\\), a circled plus marks a target that is updated by XOR, and a vertical line joins the parts of one gate. CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin preserve their full inputs in reversible form. Each drawing shows one gate, with time running left to right. Problems Apply the Toffoli gate to all eight inputs \\((a,b,c)\\). Which inputs change? Using Toffoli gates and extra bits set to fixed values, compute \\(a\\land b\\), \\(\\neg a\\), and \\(a\\oplus b\\). Specify the inputs of each gate. Prove that every output bit of a circuit of CNOT and NOT gates is a sum modulo two of input bits, possibly plus \\(1\\). Conclude that such circuits cannot compute \\(a\\land b\\)."}, {"title": "Workspace and uncomputation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Workspace and uncomputation § Toffoli gates compute every function reversibly, but a longer computation produces intermediate results along the way. What becomes of them? Compute the AND of three bits \\(x,y,z\\) with Toffoli gates, using two extra bits that start at \\(0\\): a workspace bit \\(w\\) and an output bit \\(t\\). The first Toffoli gate writes \\(w=xy\\); the second, with controls \\(w\\) and \\(z\\), writes \\(t=xyz\\): \\[(x,y,z,0,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,0)\\to(x,y,z,xy,xyz).\\] The answer is in \\(t\\), but \\(w\\) still holds \\(xy\\). What should we do with it? Resetting \\(w\\) to \\(0\\) erases it, with the cost of erasure. Leaving it means that the workspace keeps a record of the computation’s history, and every further computation adds more. There is a third option: apply the first Toffoli gate again. It is its own inverse, so it returns \\(w\\) to \\(xy\\oplus xy=0\\): \\[(x,y,z,xy,xyz)\\to(x,y,z,0,xyz).\\] This is uncomputation . In general, a reversible computation of \\(f\\) produces its answer together with intermediate values \\(g(x)\\), often called garbage. Copy the answer into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then run the computation backward: \\[(x,0,0;0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x);f(x))\\to(x,0,0;f(x)).\\] Only the input and the copied answer remain. Why go to this trouble? Classically, it avoids the cost of erasure. Quantum computation adds a stronger reason: leftover records of a computation’s history prevent the interference on which quantum algorithms depend. Compute the answer, copy its value to a fresh output, then apply the inverse computation. \\(A\\) may act on a larger workspace than the two schematic wires shown. Problems In the three-bit AND computation with workspace \\(w\\) and output \\(t\\), list the five bits \\((x,y,z,w,t)\\) after each of the three Toffoli gates for the input \\((1,1,0)\\). Compute the AND of four bits \\(x,y,z,u\\) with Toffoli gates, two workspace bits that start at \\(0\\), and one output bit, and return the workspace to \\(0\\). List the gates in order. A reversible computation \\(A\\) maps \\((x,0,0)\\) to \\((x,f(x),g(x))\\). Show that applying \\(A\\), then copying the answer register into a fresh register with CNOT gates, then applying \\(A^{-1}\\), produces \\((x,0,0;f(x))\\) for every input \\(x\\)."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto(x\\lor y,\\;x\\land y)\\) reversible? No. The inputs \\(01\\) and \\(10\\) both give \\((1,0)\\). Two output bits are not enough when the map merges inputs."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Compute the three-bit majority function reversibly: using three Toffoli gates, turn \\((x,y,z,t)\\) into \\((x,y,z,\\,t\\oplus\\mathrm{maj}(x,y,z))\\). Check the construction on the inputs \\(110\\) and \\(100\\) with \\(t=0\\). Apply Toffoli gates with controls \\((x,y)\\), \\((y,z)\\), and \\((x,z)\\), each targeting \\(t\\). Together they add \\(xy\\oplus yz\\oplus xz\\), the polynomial of majority. On \\(110\\): \\(1\\oplus0\\oplus0=1\\). On \\(100\\): \\(0\\oplus0\\oplus0=0\\). The inputs are kept, so the map is reversible; it is its own inverse."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-3", "text": "Build the reversible embedding of OR, \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t\\oplus(x\\lor y))\\), from CNOT and Toffoli gates, and show that the circuit is its own inverse. CNOT from \\(x\\) to \\(t\\), CNOT from \\(y\\) to \\(t\\), and Toffoli from \\((x,y)\\) to \\(t\\) add \\(x\\oplus y\\oplus xy=x\\lor y\\) to \\(t\\). Since none of the gates changes \\(x\\) or \\(y\\), running the circuit twice adds \\(x\\lor y\\) twice, which cancels. The circuit is its own inverse, and with \\(t=0\\) it leaves the answer in \\(t\\)."}, {"title": "Deterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 Deterministic transitions § Let us begin with the smallest computation we can examine completely: a register holding one bit, updated step by step by a rule. What must we record in order to continue such a computation? Only the value of the bit. The register therefore has two configurations , \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). A transition rule tells us, for each configuration, which configuration comes next. Consider two rules: NOT flips the bit, and RESET writes \\(0\\) whatever the bit holds. Configuration Next under NOT Next under RESET \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(1\\) \\(0\\) \\(0\\) Start from \\(0\\) and apply NOT twice. The bit goes to \\(1\\), then back to \\(0\\). Such a sequence of configurations is a run , written \\(c_0,c_1,c_2\\), where \\(c_t\\) is the configuration after \\(t\\) steps. Under RESET, the run from \\(0\\) is \\(0,0,0\\), and the run from \\(1\\) is \\(1,0,0\\). A rule is easier to study when we draw it. In a state diagram , each configuration is a circle, and each transition the rule allows is an arrow. We attach a number to each arrow, its weight . For NOT and RESET every weight is \\(1\\): the step certainly follows that arrow. Other kinds of rule will need other weights. A state diagram shows what can happen in one step, but not how a run unfolds in time. For that, draw a copy of the configurations for each time \\(t=0,1,2\\), and connect \\(i\\) at time \\(t\\) to \\(j\\) at time \\(t+1\\) whenever the rule has the arrow \\(i\\to j\\). This is the time-expanded diagram . A run is now a path through it, from time \\(0\\) to time \\(2\\). How many paths leave the start? For NOT, only one, because a single arrow leaves each configuration. A rule with exactly one outgoing arrow at every configuration is deterministic . Can we also run the computation backward? Knowing that NOT ended at \\(0\\), we know it was at \\(1\\) a step earlier, because only one arrow enters \\(0\\). Under RESET we cannot tell: arrows from \\(0\\) and from \\(1\\) both enter \\(0\\). A deterministic rule is reversible when, in addition, exactly one arrow enters each configuration. NOT: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The only path is \\(0\\to1\\to0\\). Problems On two bits, the rule SWAP exchanges the bits: \\(01\\to10\\) and \\(10\\to01\\), while \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) stay as they are. Write the two-step run from \\(01\\). Is SWAP deterministic? Is it reversible? On the configurations \\(0,1,2,3\\), the rule INC adds \\(1\\) modulo \\(4\\), and the rule HALVE replaces \\(n\\) by \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), the integer part of \\(n/2\\). Draw both state diagrams, write the two-step runs from \\(3\\), and decide which rule is reversible. Prove that a deterministic rule on finitely many configurations is reversible exactly when every configuration has at least one incoming arrow. Then show that the statement fails for the rule \\(n\\mapsto\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) on all nonnegative integers."}, {"title": "Nondeterministic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 Nondeterministic transitions § What if a rule allows more than one next configuration? Consider KEEP-OR-FLIP: after either configuration, both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are allowed. Its state diagram has all four arrows, which we label “yes.” A rule of this kind is nondeterministic . Starting from \\(0\\), how many runs are there? Two arrows leave the start, and two leave each configuration after that, so four paths reach time \\(2\\): \\[0\\to0\\to0,\\qquad0\\to1\\to0,\\qquad0\\to0\\to1,\\qquad0\\to1\\to1.\\] The rule does not say which path is taken. What can we still ask? Whether a run is possible : it is when every arrow on its path is allowed. And whether a configuration is reachable : it is when some possible path ends there. Here both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are reachable after two steps. Two paths reach \\(0\\). Does that make \\(0\\) twice as likely? No. The rule lists what is allowed and says nothing about likelihood. Nondeterminism is still a useful idea: a nondeterministic machine accepts an input if some possible run accepts it, and Chapter 9 defines the class NP in this way. To speak about how likely an outcome is, the arrows need numbers of another kind. KEEP-OR-FLIP: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Four paths are possible; both configurations are reachable at time \\(2\\). Problems A rule on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) allows \\(0\\to1\\), \\(1\\to0\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), and forbids \\(0\\to0\\). List the possible two-step runs from \\(0\\), and the configurations reachable at time \\(2\\). For the same rule, list the possible three-step runs from \\(0\\). Which configurations are reachable at time \\(3\\), and by how many paths? On the configurations \\(0,1,\\dots,k\\), a rule allows \\(i\\to i\\) and \\(i\\to i+1\\) for \\(i\\lt k\\), and only \\(k\\to k\\) at \\(k\\). Show that \\(k\\) is reachable from \\(0\\) at time \\(t\\) exactly when \\(t\\geq k\\), and that the number of paths from \\(0\\) to \\(k\\) at time \\(t\\) is \\(\\binom tk\\)."}, {"title": "Probabilistic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Probabilistic transitions § A nondeterministic rule says which configurations may come next, but not how often each does. Suppose the rule chooses at random. Each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) then carries a probability \\(P(j\\mid i)\\), read “the probability of \\(j\\) given \\(i\\)”: the probability that the next configuration is \\(j\\) when the current one is \\(i\\). Some next configuration must occur, so the probabilities on the arrows leaving a configuration add to \\(1\\). Such a rule is probabilistic . Our example is COIN: whatever the bit holds, replace it by a fair coin flip. Every arrow carries \\(1/2\\). What is the probability that a run from \\(0\\) ends at \\(0\\)? Take one path, \\(0\\to1\\to0\\). The first arrow is followed with probability \\(1/2\\). Given that it was, the second is followed with probability \\(1/2\\). The path therefore has probability \\(1/2\\cdot1/2=1/4\\). We have used that each step depends only on the current configuration, not on how the run reached it; a process with this property is a Markov process . Two paths end at \\(0\\). A run follows exactly one path, so these alternatives cannot both happen, and their probabilities add: \\[P(c_2=0)=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12.\\] Likewise \\(P(c_2=1)=1/2\\). A fair coin flipped twice is still fair. What does a probability tell us about a single run? Nothing definite: a single run ends at \\(0\\) or at \\(1\\). The probability predicts how often each happens when the run is repeated many times. COIN: state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). Each path has probability \\(1/4\\); two paths end at each configuration. Problems A step keeps the bit with probability \\(2/3\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/3\\). Starting from \\(0\\), compute \\(P(c_2=0)\\) by listing the paths that end at \\(0\\). For the same rule, compute \\(P(c_3=0)\\) from \\(0\\). Check your answer against the formula \\(P(c_t=0)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^t\\). Prove the formula \\(P(c_t=0)=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\left(\\tfrac13\\right)^t\\) for the rule above, and describe what happens to the run’s memory of its starting configuration as \\(t\\) grows."}, {"title": "The product rule along a path", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 The product rule along a path § Look back at the three calculations. Each time we did two things: we combined the arrows along a path, and then we combined the paths ending at the same configuration. Can one recipe cover all three kinds of rule? Give every arrow a weight: \\(1\\) for an arrow of a deterministic or nondeterministic rule, \\(P(j\\mid i)\\) for an arrow of a probabilistic rule, and \\(0\\) for an arrow the rule does not have. Along a path, multiply. Product rule. The weight of a path is the product of the weights of its arrows: \\[w(i\\to j\\to k)=w(j\\to k)\\,w(i\\to j).\\] Does this give the right answer each time? For a deterministic rule, the product is \\(1\\) along the run and \\(0\\) on any path that uses a missing arrow. For a nondeterministic rule, a product of ones and zeros is \\(1\\) exactly when every arrow on the path is allowed. For a probabilistic rule, it is the probability of the path, as we found for COIN. Under COIN, every path of \\(n\\) steps has weight \\((1/2)^n\\). Multiplying the weights along the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\) gives its weight in each model. Problems Under the rule that keeps the bit with probability \\(2/3\\) and flips it with probability \\(1/3\\), what is the weight of the path \\(0\\to0\\to1\\to1\\)? A nondeterministic rule on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) allows \\(0\\to0\\), \\(0\\to1\\), and \\(1\\to1\\), and forbids \\(1\\to0\\). Using the product rule with weights \\(1\\) and \\(0\\), decide which of the eight three-step paths from \\(0\\) are possible. The product rule for probabilities assumes the Markov property. Find a two-step random process on \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) in which the probability of the path \\(0\\to1\\to0\\), given the start \\(0\\), differs from \\(P(c_1=1\\mid c_0=0)\\,P(c_2=0\\mid c_1=1)\\)."}, {"title": "The sum rule across paths", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 The sum rule across paths § Now combine the paths. Several paths can end at the same configuration, and we add their weights. Sum rule. The weight of reaching \\(k\\) from \\(i\\) is the sum of the weights of all paths from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\). In two steps the paths differ only in the intermediate configuration \\(j\\), so \\[w(i\\Rightarrow k)=\\sum_j w(j\\to k)\\,w(i\\to j),\\] where the sum runs over all configurations \\(j\\). For a probabilistic rule this is the calculation we made for COIN: \\[P(k\\mid i)=\\sum_jP(k\\mid j)\\,P(j\\mid i)=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12.\\] What does the sum mean for the other kinds of rule? For a deterministic rule, at most one path has weight \\(1\\), so the sum is \\(1\\) if the run ends at \\(k\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. For a nondeterministic rule, the sum counts the possible paths, and \\(k\\) is reachable when the count is positive. Could adding a path ever lower the weight of reaching a configuration? Not with any of these rules: none of their weights is negative, so every path adds something. Adding the weights of the paths that end at \\(0\\) gives the weight of ending there. Problems Under KEEP-OR-FLIP, how many possible paths lead from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) in three steps, and what does the count tell us? A rule has \\(P(0\\mid0)=2/3\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/3\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\). Use the sum rule to compute the four two-step weights \\(w(i\\Rightarrow k)\\), and check that the weights from each start add to \\(1\\). Prove that, for any probabilistic rule, the two-step weights \\(w(i\\Rightarrow k)\\) given by the sum and product rules add to \\(1\\) over \\(k\\). What does this show about composing two probabilistic steps?"}, {"title": "States and steps", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 States and steps § So far each run started from a definite configuration. What if we know the configuration only with some probability? We then describe the register by a state : a weight \\(v_j\\) for each configuration \\(j\\). The definite configuration \\(i\\) is the state with weight \\(1\\) on \\(i\\) and \\(0\\) elsewhere; a probability distribution is a state whose weights are nonnegative and add to \\(1\\). How does one step change a state? The weight \\(v_i\\) travels along each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) and is multiplied by \\(w(i\\to j)\\), by the product rule. At \\(j\\), the arriving contributions add, by the sum rule: \\[v^\\prime_j=\\sum_i w(i\\to j)\\,v_i.\\] Under COIN, every probability distribution goes to the state with weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\). When can a step be undone, so that a second rule brings every state back to where it started? For a deterministic rule, exactly when one arrow leaves and one arrow enters each configuration; reversing every arrow undoes it. The reversible gates are of this kind. CNOT, for example, acts on two bits by the arrows \\(00\\to00\\), \\(01\\to01\\), \\(10\\to11\\), and \\(11\\to10\\). RESET and COIN send different states to the same state. Nothing can undo them, because the result does not record where they started. COIN sends every distribution to the state with weights \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\), so the state after the step does not determine the state before it. Problems Apply one step of COIN, and separately one step of NOT, to the state with weights \\(0.9\\) on \\(0\\) and \\(0.1\\) on \\(1\\). For the rule with \\(P(0\\mid0)=2/3\\), \\(P(1\\mid0)=1/3\\), \\(P(0\\mid1)=1/4\\), and \\(P(1\\mid1)=3/4\\), find the distribution that one step leaves unchanged. Prove that a probabilistic rule on finitely many configurations can be undone by another probabilistic rule only if it is deterministic and reversible."}, {"title": "An operation that probability cannot describe", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 An operation that probability cannot describe § The bits in a computer are held by devices made of very many atoms. What happens when a bit is carried by a single atom? Choose two distinguishable conditions of the atom, such as two of its energy levels, and label them \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). A two-state system of this kind is a qubit , short for quantum bit. Looking at it, which is called a measurement , gives one of the two labels. Experiments with qubits reveal an operation, which we call \\(H\\), with the following behaviour. Applied once to \\(0\\) or to \\(1\\) and then measured, it gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). Applied twice to \\(0\\) and then measured, it gives \\(0\\) with certainty; applied twice to \\(1\\), it gives \\(1\\) with certainty. Applied, measured, applied again, and measured, it gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). Observation 1 looks familiar: one application of \\(H\\) behaves like COIN. Observation 2 does not. Can the rules we already have describe it? To put the question precisely, we assume as little as possible. Assumption 1. Each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) of \\(H\\) has a weight \\(H(i\\to j)\\), and repeated applications compose by the product and sum rules. The probability of a measurement outcome is \\(f(w)\\), where \\(w\\) is the weight of reaching that outcome, and \\(f(w)=0\\) exactly when \\(w=0\\). Proposition. No nonnegative weights satisfy observations 1 and 2. Proof. By observation 1, outcome \\(1\\) has nonzero probability after one step from \\(0\\) and after one step from \\(1\\), so \\(H(0\\to1)\\) and \\(H(1\\to1)\\) are nonzero, hence positive. The two paths from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) give the weight \\(H(0\\to0)H(0\\to1)+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)\\geq H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)\\gt 0\\). Outcome \\(1\\) then has nonzero probability, contradicting observation 2. \\(\\square\\) If the product and sum rules hold at all, then, some weight of \\(H\\) must be negative, and the two paths from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) must cancel. Observation 3 adds a clue: when the qubit is measured between the steps, they do not cancel. Quantum mechanics is the physical theory of systems such as qubits. We now find its rules for a qubit, starting from Assumption 1 and the three observations. COIN matches a single application and an interrupted pair, but not two unobserved applications. The unobserved qubit keeps information that the probabilities do not. Problems Which of observations 1, 2, and 3 does COIN reproduce? Suppose a probabilistic rule satisfies observation 1. Show that it must be COIN, and compute the probability that two steps from \\(0\\) end at \\(1\\). The argument against a probabilistic description of \\(H\\) used runs starting from both \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). Why would the run from \\(0\\) alone not rule out every probabilistic rule?"}, {"title": "From weights to probabilities", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 From weights to probabilities § Once weights can be negative, a weight is no longer a probability. How, then, do we obtain probabilities from weights? First some notation. The state of a qubit is a weight for each configuration, \\(v_0\\) and \\(v_1\\). We write it \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\), where \\(\\ket0\\), read “ket zero,” stands for the definite configuration \\(0\\), and \\(\\ket1\\) for \\(1\\). When both weights are nonzero, the state is a superposition . What must the function \\(f\\) satisfy? A probability is never negative, so \\(f\\) must be nonnegative even for negative weights. And the probabilities of the two outcomes must add to \\(1\\) for every state an operation can produce. The simplest candidates are powers of the magnitude. Assumption 2. \\(f(w)=|w|^p\\) for some \\(p\\gt 0\\). Every operation must then leave \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^p\\) unchanged, whatever the state. Which operations do that? For most values of \\(p\\), very few. Theorem (quoted without proof). For \\(p\\neq2\\), the only operations that leave \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^p\\) unchanged for every state are those in which exactly one arrow leaves and exactly one arrow enters each configuration, with weight \\(+1\\) or \\(-1\\). Such an operation sends each configuration to a single configuration, so it can never produce the coin-like result of observation 1. Only \\(p=2\\) remains. Measurement rule. Measuring the state \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(v_j^2\\), and \\(v_0^2+v_1^2=1\\). What does this demand of an operation? It must leave the total squared weight unchanged for every state. Start from the definite configuration \\(i\\): after one step the weights are \\(w(i\\to j)\\), so their squares must add to \\(1\\). Now start from the state with weight \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) on \\(i\\) and on another configuration \\(k\\). After one step, \\(j\\) has weight \\(\\bigl(w(i\\to j)+w(k\\to j)\\bigr)/\\sqrt2\\), and the total squared weight works out to \\(1+\\sum_jw(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)\\). For this to be \\(1\\), the last sum must vanish. So every operation satisfies \\[\\sum_j w(i\\to j)^2=1,\\qquad \\sum_j w(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\quad(i\\neq k).\\] These conditions have a consequence: every operation can be undone. Reverse each arrow and keep its weight. Applying the operation and then this reverse, the paths from \\(i\\) to \\(k\\) give the weight \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)\\), which is \\(1\\) when \\(k=i\\) and \\(0\\) otherwise. Every configuration returns to where it started. Which of the rules we know qualify? A reversible deterministic rule satisfies both conditions, so every reversible gate is a valid operation on qubits. COIN does not: the squares of the weights leaving \\(0\\) add to \\(1/2\\). Nor does RESET: applied to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), it gives the weight \\(1/\\sqrt2-1/\\sqrt2=0\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(0\\) at \\(1\\), so no outcome has any probability. With probability \\(|w|^p\\), every operation must preserve \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^p\\). Only \\(p=2\\) allows an operation that sends a configuration to an equal superposition. Problems Which of the following are possible qubit states under the measurement rule: \\(\\tfrac12\\ket0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\); \\(\\tfrac35\\ket0-\\tfrac45\\ket1\\); \\(\\ket0-\\ket1\\)? An operation has weights \\(w(0\\to0)=\\tfrac35\\), \\(w(0\\to1)=\\tfrac45\\), \\(w(1\\to0)=\\tfrac45\\), and \\(w(1\\to1)=-\\tfrac35\\). Check the two conditions on an operation, and apply it to \\(\\ket0\\) and to \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Derive the condition \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\) for \\(i\\neq k\\) from the requirement that every operation leaves the total squared weight unchanged."}, {"title": "The Hadamard step", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-3", "text": "3.3.3 The Hadamard step § We can now find the weights of \\(H\\) itself. Observation 1 says that one step from either configuration gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\). By the measurement rule, every weight has square \\(1/2\\), so each is \\(\\pm1/\\sqrt2\\). Which signs? Observation 2 decides. Two steps must return each configuration, so the paths from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) must cancel, and the paths from \\(0\\) to \\(0\\) must add to \\(1\\): \\[H(0\\to0)H(0\\to1)+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)=0,\\qquad H(0\\to0)^2+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to0)=1.\\] Since \\(H(0\\to1)\\neq0\\), the first equation gives \\(H(1\\to1)=-H(0\\to0)\\). The second gives \\(H(0\\to1)H(1\\to0)=1/2\\), so \\(H(0\\to1)=H(1\\to0)\\). The signs of \\(H(0\\to0)\\) and \\(H(0\\to1)\\) are left free; choosing both positive, \\[H(0\\to0)=H(0\\to1)=H(1\\to0)=\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\qquad H(1\\to1)=-\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\] so that \\[H\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad H\\ket1=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Are these weights a valid operation? The squares leaving each configuration add to \\(1/2+1/2=1\\), and \\(H(0\\to0)H(1\\to0)+H(0\\to1)H(1\\to1)=1/2-1/2=0\\). They are. Now the cancellation can be seen directly. Each two-step path from \\(0\\) has weight \\(\\pm1/2\\), and \\[\\text{weight of reaching }0=\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12=1,\\qquad\\text{weight of reaching }1=\\tfrac12-\\tfrac12=0.\\] The paths to \\(1\\) cancel because the arrow \\(1\\to1\\) carries \\(-1/\\sqrt2\\). The arrows of COIN have the same size, but none is negative, so nothing can cancel. \\(H\\): state diagram (left) and time-expanded diagram from \\(0\\) (right). The paths to \\(0\\) add to weight \\(1\\); the paths to \\(1\\) cancel. Problems Apply \\(H\\) to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) using the weights of its arrows. Draw the time-expanded diagram of two Hadamard steps from \\(1\\). Compute the path weights to \\(0\\) and to \\(1\\), and the final probabilities. Observation 2 left the signs of \\(H(0\\to0)\\) and \\(H(0\\to1)\\) free. Write the four resulting operations, and show that each satisfies observations 1 and 2. Which of them differ from \\(H\\) only by a factor common to all weights?"}, {"title": "Complex weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Complex weights § Are negative weights enough? Ask a simple question: is there an operation that, applied twice, acts as NOT, half of a NOT? There must be one if operations come from physical processes that run continuously in time: stop the process halfway. Assumption 3. Every operation has a square root: an operation that, applied twice, equals it. NOT is a valid operation, since it is a reversible gate. Suppose \\(S\\) has real weights and two steps of \\(S\\) give NOT. The paths from \\(0\\) and from \\(1\\) give \\[S(0\\to0)^2+S(0\\to1)S(1\\to0)=0,\\qquad S(0\\to1)\\bigl(S(0\\to0)+S(1\\to1)\\bigr)=1,\\] \\[S(1\\to1)^2+S(1\\to0)S(0\\to1)=0,\\qquad S(1\\to0)\\bigl(S(0\\to0)+S(1\\to1)\\bigr)=1.\\] The two equations on the right show that \\(S(0\\to1)\\) and \\(S(1\\to0)\\) are nonzero with the same sign, so their product is positive. The first equation then asks for \\(S(0\\to0)^2\\lt 0\\). No real number has a negative square, so real weights are not enough. We need numbers whose squares can be negative. A complex number is \\(z=a+ib\\) with \\(a,b\\) real and \\(i^2=-1\\). Its conjugate is \\(z^*=a-ib\\), and its magnitude \\(|z|\\) satisfies \\(|z|^2=z^*z=a^2+b^2\\). In polar form \\(z=re^{i\\phi}\\), with \\(r=|z|\\), phase \\(\\phi\\), and \\(e^{i\\phi}=\\cos\\phi+i\\sin\\phi\\). Multiplication multiplies magnitudes and adds phases; \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\) have phases \\(0\\) and \\(\\pi\\). Complex weights are called amplitudes . What changes in our rules? A probability must be real and nonnegative, so in the measurement rule the square becomes the squared magnitude. Measurement rule. Measuring the state \\(v_0\\ket0+v_1\\ket1\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|v_j|^2\\), and \\(|v_0|^2+|v_1|^2=1\\). Repeating the argument of the previous section with magnitudes, every operation satisfies \\[\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2=1,\\qquad \\sum_j w(i\\to j)^*\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\quad(i\\neq k),\\] and is undone by reversing every arrow and conjugating its weight. An operation satisfying these conditions is unitary . And half of a NOT now exists. Take \\[S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=\\frac{1+i}2,\\qquad S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=\\frac{1-i}2.\\] It is unitary, and two steps of \\(S\\) give NOT: from \\(0\\), the weight of reaching \\(0\\) is \\(\\tfrac14\\bigl((1+i)^2+(1-i)^2\\bigr)=0\\), and the weight of reaching \\(1\\) is \\(\\tfrac14(1-i)\\cdot2(1+i)=1\\). The four kinds of rule now sit side by side. Along and across paths the arithmetic is the same; only the kind of weight changes. Rule Weight of an arrow Along a path Across paths Preserved by each step Deterministic \\(1\\); one arrow leaves each configuration Multiply At most one path One definite configuration Nondeterministic \\(1\\) for each allowed arrow Multiply Count None Probabilistic \\(P(j\\mid i)\\) Multiply Add \\(\\sum_jv_j\\) Quantum Complex amplitude Multiply Add; probability \\(=|\\text{amplitude}|^2\\) \\(\\sum_j|v_j|^2\\) The complex numbers \\(1\\), \\(i\\), \\(-1\\), and \\(-i\\) have magnitude one and phases \\(0\\), \\(\\pi/2\\), \\(\\pi\\), and \\(3\\pi/2\\). Multiplying complex numbers adds their phases. Problems Which two-step path sums show that an operation with real weights cannot equal NOT when applied twice? For the operation \\(S\\) with \\(S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=(1+i)/2\\) and \\(S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=(1-i)/2\\), compute the two-step weights from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\) and to \\(1\\). Find every operation with \\(S(0\\to0)=S(1\\to1)=a\\) and \\(S(0\\to1)=S(1\\to0)=b\\) whose two steps give NOT. Show in particular that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) cannot both be real."}, {"title": "Interference and phase", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Interference and phase § Two paths to the same outcome can now carry amplitudes such as \\(1/2\\) and \\(i/2\\), which neither reinforce nor cancel completely. What, in general, decides the probability of an outcome? When contributions to the same outcome reinforce one another, they interfere constructively ; when they cancel, partly or completely, they interfere destructively . In either case the probability of an outcome \\(k\\) is \\[p(k)=\\Bigl|\\sum_{\\text{paths to }k}\\text{path amplitude}\\Bigr|^2.\\] What if we squared each path first? For two applications of \\(H\\) from \\(0\\), we would get \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\) for each outcome, which is exactly the prediction of COIN. The order matters: add, then square. Did the qubit pass through \\(0\\) or through \\(1\\) between the steps? The calculation does not say. Had it been in a definite configuration, we would see observation 3, not observation 2. The intermediate configuration is a label we sum over, not an event that took place. Paths whose amplitudes are added in this way are coherent alternatives . The probabilities still add to \\(1\\), so interference that raises one outcome lowers another. This is what a quantum algorithm exploits: it arranges its operations so that paths to correct answers reinforce and paths to wrong answers cancel. Which features of a state actually matter? Multiply every amplitude by the same \\(e^{i\\theta}\\). By the sum and product rules every later amplitude is multiplied by the same factor, and \\(|e^{i\\theta}z|=|z|\\), so no probability ever changes. Such a factor is a global phase , and states that differ by one are physically the same. A difference of phase between amplitudes, a relative phase , is another matter. Take \\[\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1\\bigr).\\] Measured directly, it gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) for every \\(\\phi\\). After \\(H\\), the two paths to \\(0\\) give the amplitude \\((1+e^{i\\phi})/2\\), so \\(p(0)=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). The relative phase is invisible at first and decisive one step later. The operation \\(Z\\), with \\(Z(0\\to0)=1\\) and \\(Z(1\\to1)=-1\\), shifts the relative phase by \\(\\pi\\). Applying \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\), while applying \\(H\\) twice gives \\(\\ket0\\). Check your understanding What goes wrong if path amplitudes are squared before they are added? Compare your explanation Squaring first discards the phases, so nothing can cancel. The result is the probabilistic prediction, which assigns a nonzero probability to an outcome whose amplitudes cancel. The first two states differ only by a global sign and have the same output probabilities. Changing only one component changes the outcome after \\(H\\). Problems Two paths reach an outcome with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(i/2\\). What is its probability? Is the interference constructive, destructive, or neither? Two paths contribute \\(0.6\\) and \\(-0.2\\) to an outcome. Compare the probability obtained by adding first with the result of squaring first. After \\(H\\), the state \\((\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) with probability \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Which phases cannot be told apart this way, and what further operation would separate them?"}, {"title": "Path information and interference", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 Path information and interference § Measuring the qubit between the two steps destroys the cancellation. Does it take a measurement? Suppose instead that a second bit quietly copies the path. Let this record start at \\(0\\), and write two-bit configurations with the qubit first. Insert CNOT, with the qubit as control and the record as target, between the two applications of \\(H\\). \\(H\\) acts on the qubit only: \\[\\ket{00}\\xrightarrow{H}\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}\\bigr)\\xrightarrow{\\text{CNOT}}\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}\\bigr)\\xrightarrow{H}\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11}\\bigr).\\] Look at the amplitudes for qubit outcome \\(1\\): still \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). They now belong to different configurations, \\(10\\) and \\(11\\), because the record differs. The sum rule adds only paths that end in the same configuration, so nothing cancels, and the qubit gives \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\). Nobody looked at the record; its existence is enough. Can the damage be repaired? Apply CNOT once more, before the final \\(H\\). The record returns to \\(0\\) on both paths, the state is again \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), and the final \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket{00}\\). This is uncomputation , here used to restore interference. In general it is a matter of degree. Identical records keep the interference, perfectly distinguishable records destroy it, and partly distinguishable records reduce it; Chapter 5 makes this precise. The practical lesson for quantum computation: any workspace that records which path was taken must be uncomputed before the paths are brought together. Copying the intermediate configuration into a record separates the paths, so their amplitudes no longer meet. Uncomputing the record restores the interference. Problems In the record experiment, find the probability of each of the four final configurations, and the probability that the record shows \\(1\\). Check the final state \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})\\) by applying \\(H\\) to the qubit in \\(\\ket{00}\\) and in \\(\\ket{11}\\) separately. Replace the CNOT by an operation that records the path only partly: it leaves \\(\\ket{00}\\) unchanged and sends \\(\\ket{10}\\) to \\(\\ket1(\\cos\\alpha\\ket0+\\sin\\alpha\\ket1)\\). Show that the qubit then gives \\(1\\) at the end with probability \\((1-\\cos\\alpha)/2\\), and interpret the cases \\(\\alpha=0\\) and \\(\\alpha=\\pi/2\\)."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two paths reach an outcome with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(e^{i\\pi/3}/2\\). What is its probability? \\(|1+e^{i\\pi/3}|^2/4=(2+2\\cos60^\\circ)/4=3/4\\). Squaring each path first would give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\): the phases partly reinforce."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-2", "text": "An operation \\(G\\) on a qubit has the real weights \\(G(0\\to0)=G(1\\to1)=\\cos\\alpha\\), \\(G(0\\to1)=\\sin\\alpha\\), and \\(G(1\\to0)=-\\sin\\alpha\\). Check the two conditions on an operation, compute the two-step weights by path sums, and find the \\(\\alpha\\) for which two steps send \\(0\\) to \\(1\\). The squares leaving each configuration add to \\(\\cos^2\\alpha+\\sin^2\\alpha=1\\), and the cross sum is \\(-\\cos\\alpha\\sin\\alpha+\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha=0\\). From \\(0\\): weight \\(\\cos^2\\alpha-\\sin^2\\alpha=\\cos2\\alpha\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha=\\sin2\\alpha\\) at \\(1\\). From \\(1\\): \\(-\\sin2\\alpha\\) at \\(0\\) and \\(\\cos2\\alpha\\) at \\(1\\). At \\(\\alpha=\\pi/4\\), two steps send \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket1\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to \\(-\\ket0\\): NOT, except for a relative sign. Real weights reach NOT only up to this sign; exactly NOT needs complex weights."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-3", "text": "Apply \\(H\\), then the phase step with \\(w(0\\to0)=1\\) and \\(w(1\\to1)=e^{i\\phi}\\), then \\(H\\), to \\(\\ket0\\). Using path sums, find the probability of outcome \\(0\\) as a function of \\(\\phi\\), and the values of \\(\\phi\\) that make each outcome certain. To \\(0\\): \\(\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12e^{i\\phi}\\). To \\(1\\): \\(\\tfrac12-\\tfrac12e^{i\\phi}\\). The probability of \\(0\\) is \\(|1+e^{i\\phi}|^2/4=(1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). Outcome \\(0\\) is certain at \\(\\phi=0\\) and outcome \\(1\\) at \\(\\phi=\\pi\\). A phase that no direct measurement sees controls the result completely."}, {"title": "State vectors and ket notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 State vectors and ket notation § The state of a qubit is a pair of amplitudes, one for each configuration. A system with more configurations, such as two qubits with configurations \\(00,01,10,11\\), has one amplitude for each of them. Can we treat such a list as a single object? We can, as a column vector. List the configurations in a fixed order and write the amplitudes in that order. For a qubit, \\[\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}\\alpha\\\\\\beta\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\ket0=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\\ket1=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] The bracket in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) names a vector; it is not an operation. This is Dirac notation . The vectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) of the definite configurations form the computational basis . Every state is a combination of them, and the measurement rule reads its probabilities from the coordinates: \\(|\\alpha|^2\\) and \\(|\\beta|^2\\). The coordinates of a state vector therefore satisfy \\(|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1\\). Is “being in a superposition ” a property of the state alone? No. The state \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) is a superposition of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), but it is itself a vector of another basis, as we will see. A superposition is always a superposition relative to some basis. With \\(n\\) qubits there are \\(2^n\\) configurations, so a state vector has \\(2^n\\) coordinates. The two coordinates are ordered by the selected basis. The coefficients belong to basis vectors, not to two simultaneous measurement outcomes. Problems Write \\(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt3}\\ket0+i\\sqrt{\\tfrac23}\\ket1\\) as a column vector, and check that its coordinates satisfy the condition for a state vector. With the two-qubit configurations ordered \\(00,01,10,11\\), write \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\) as a column vector, and give the probability of each configuration. Find numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) with \\(\\ket0=a\\,\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}+b\\,\\tfrac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\). Relative to which basis is \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) a superposition, and relative to which is it not?"}, {"title": "Operations as matrices", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Operations as matrices § Applying an operation by path sums means writing out every arrow. Is there a more compact way? Collect the weights in a table whose entry in row \\(j\\) and column \\(i\\) is the weight of the arrow \\(i\\to j\\). This table is a matrix \\(U\\), with \\(U_{ji}=w(i\\to j)\\). One step changes the state by \\(v^\\prime_j=\\sum_iw(i\\to j)\\,v_i\\). In matrix language this is \\(v^\\prime_j=\\sum_iU_{ji}v_i\\), the matrix–vector product: \\(\\ket{\\psi^\\prime}=U\\ket\\psi\\). The sum and product rules for one step are exactly matrix multiplication. Column \\(i\\) of \\(U\\) holds the weights of the arrows leaving \\(i\\), so it is the state \\(U\\ket i\\). For the operations we have met, writing \\(X\\) for NOT and \\(\\operatorname{diag}(a,b)\\) for the matrix with diagonal entries \\(a,b\\) and zeros elsewhere, \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1),\\qquad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Two steps, first \\(U\\) and then \\(V\\), sum over the intermediate configuration \\(j\\): \\((VU)_{ki}=\\sum_jV_{kj}U_{ji}\\). This is the matrix product, with the rightmost matrix acting first. For example, \\(HH=I\\), the identity matrix. Why is a matrix determined by what it does to the basis states? Because \\(U(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha\\,U\\ket0+\\beta\\,U\\ket1\\): each configuration’s amplitude is carried along separately and the results add. This property, linearity , is the sum rule in another form. Which matrices are valid operations? The two conditions on the weights of an operation decide, and their compact form needs one more tool. The \\(X\\) operation is fixed by its two basis actions. Multiply those outputs by the input coefficients and add to obtain the output on a superposition. Problems An operation has weights \\(w(0\\to0)=0\\), \\(w(0\\to1)=i\\), \\(w(1\\to0)=-i\\), and \\(w(1\\to1)=0\\). Write its matrix, and apply it to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). Compute the matrices \\(XZ\\) and \\(ZX\\), and apply each to \\(\\ket0\\). Does the order of the operations matter? A proposed rule sends \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) to \\(\\alpha\\ket0+2\\beta\\ket1\\), rescaled so that the squared magnitudes add to \\(1\\). Show that the rule is not linear by comparing its action on \\(\\ket0\\), \\(\\ket1\\), and \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Inner products, bras, and adjoints", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Inner products, bras, and adjoints § The conditions on an operation were \\(\\sum_j|w(i\\to j)|^2=1\\) and \\(\\sum_jw(i\\to j)^*\\,w(k\\to j)=0\\) for \\(i\\neq k\\). Since \\(w(i\\to j)=U_{ji}\\), these are statements about the columns of \\(U\\): each column has squared magnitudes adding to \\(1\\), and for two different columns, the conjugated entries of one times the entries of the other add to \\(0\\). Such sums deserve a name. The inner product of two vectors is \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j.\\] Why the conjugate? So that \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\psi_j|^2\\) is real and nonnegative. Without it, the one-coordinate vector \\((i)\\) would have “squared length” \\(i^2=-1\\). The norm \\(\\|\\psi\\|=\\sqrt{\\braket\\psi\\psi}\\) is the length of \\(\\ket\\psi\\); a state vector has norm \\(1\\). Two vectors are orthogonal when their inner product is \\(0\\), and a basis of orthogonal unit vectors is orthonormal . The bra \\(\\bra\\phi\\) is the row of conjugated coordinates of \\(\\ket\\phi\\), so that the row \\(\\bra\\phi\\) times the column \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is the inner product \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). For example, \\(\\bra0=(1,0)\\), and \\(\\braket0\\psi=\\alpha\\) picks out the first coordinate. The conditions on an operation now say that the columns of \\(U\\) form an orthonormal basis. To write this as one equation, define the adjoint \\(U^\\dagger\\) of a matrix as its conjugate transpose: \\((U^\\dagger)_{ij}=U_{ji}^*\\). Then \\((U^\\dagger U)_{ik}=\\sum_jU_{ji}^*U_{jk}\\) is the inner product of columns \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), and the conditions say exactly \\[U^\\dagger U=I.\\] A matrix with this property is unitary , and \\(U^\\dagger\\) is its inverse: reverse each arrow and conjugate its weight. Is the adjoint always the inverse? No. For the matrix \\(\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\), which is not unitary, the adjoint is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\) itself, while the inverse is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,1)\\). In general the adjoint is characterized by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\): it moves a matrix from one side of an inner product to the other. For \\(v=(1,i)\\), the conjugate transpose is \\((1,-i)\\). Multiplying gives \\(1+1=2\\). Omitting conjugation would incorrectly give zero. Problems Compute \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi\\) for \\(\\ket\\phi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Are the two states orthogonal? Find the adjoints of \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and of \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). Which of the two matrices is unitary? Using \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\), prove that \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Conclude that the product of two unitaries is unitary."}, {"title": "State and evolution postulates", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 State and evolution postulates § For a qubit, the rules of quantum mechanics followed from a few observations and stated assumptions. Do they hold for every quantum system? Quantum mechanics asserts that they do. We adopt them as postulates : rules taken as the starting point of the theory and tested by experiment. Postulate 1 (states). A state of a quantum system is a unit vector in a complex vector space whose orthonormal basis corresponds to the configurations of the system. Vectors that differ only by a global phase represent the same state. Postulate 2 (evolution). An operation on a system that does not interact with anything else is a unitary matrix \\(U\\); it changes the state \\(\\ket\\psi\\) into \\(U\\ket\\psi\\). What does a general qubit state look like? Remove a global phase so that the first coordinate is real and nonnegative. The state then has the form \\[\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket1,\\] where the magnitudes fix \\(\\theta\\) and the relative phase is \\(\\phi\\). Is every preparation described by a single state vector? No. If we know only that a system was prepared in one of several states with given probabilities, or if it is part of a larger system it has interacted with, a different description is needed, the density matrix of Appendix D. The main text works with single state vectors. The vector \\((3,4)\\) has norm five. Dividing each coordinate by five gives probabilities \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\), which add to one. Problems Normalize \\(\\ket0+2i\\ket1\\), and give the probabilities of a computational-basis measurement. Write the state \\((i\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) in the form \\(\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\ket1\\), after removing a global phase. Show that \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(e^{i\\gamma}\\ket\\psi\\) give the same probabilities for every sequence of operations followed by a computational-basis measurement. Then show that \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) do not differ by a global phase."}, {"title": "The measurement postulate", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 The measurement postulate § The measurement rule gives the probability of each outcome. What is the state after the measurement? Measure a qubit and obtain \\(0\\), then measure it again immediately. Experiments find \\(0\\) again, every time. Which state gives \\(0\\) with certainty? Only \\(\\ket0\\), up to a global phase. So after the outcome \\(0\\), the qubit is in the state \\(\\ket0\\). Postulate 3 (measurement). Measuring the state \\(\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\ket j\\) in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\alpha_j|^2\\), and leaves the system in the state \\(\\ket j\\). For \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the outcome is \\(0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\); after the outcome \\(1\\), the state is \\(\\ket1\\). A single run gives a single outcome; the probabilities describe how often each outcome occurs. This also explains why measuring between two applications of \\(H\\) destroys their return to the start: after the measurement the qubit is \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket1\\), and \\(H\\) applied to either gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\). Is a measurement an operation in the sense of Postulate 2? No. When the outcome is \\(0\\), it sends both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) to \\(\\ket0\\). Different states merge, so a measurement cannot be undone and is not unitary. A measurement leaves the observed configuration, so an immediate second measurement repeats the result with certainty. Problems Measure \\((\\ket0+2i\\ket1)/\\sqrt5\\) in the computational basis. Give the probability of each outcome and the state after it. A qubit in \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) is measured twice in a row. Find the probabilities of the four sequences of outcomes \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\). Start from \\(\\ket0\\). Apply \\(H\\), measure, apply \\(H\\), and measure again. Find the probabilities of the four pairs of outcomes, and compare with applying \\(H\\) twice and measuring once."}, {"title": "Repeated measurements on independently prepared states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 Repeated measurements on independently prepared states § How can a predicted probability be tested when a single run gives a single outcome? Prepare the same state many times, independently, and measure each copy. If an outcome has probability \\(p\\), its count in \\(N\\) runs has mean \\(Np\\) and variance \\(Np(1-p)\\). The observed fraction therefore fluctuates around \\(p\\) by about \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)/N}\\), which shrinks as \\(1/\\sqrt N\\). A finite experiment estimates \\(p\\); it does not reproduce it exactly. Is measuring one system again the same as measuring a fresh copy? No. After a measurement the system is in the observed configuration, so an immediate second measurement repeats the result with certainty. Measuring \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\); measuring the same qubit again repeats that result, while a fresh copy again gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\). The counts of an experiment therefore mean something only once both the preparation and the measurement are specified. After observing zero in a computational-basis measurement, the same qubit remains in \\(\\ket0\\). A fresh \\(\\ket+\\) still gives an equal distribution. Problems A preparation gives outcome \\(1\\) with probability \\(0.3\\). In \\(100\\) independent runs, what is the expected number of \\(1\\)s, and its standard deviation \\(\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}\\)? For \\(p=1/2\\), how many independent runs are needed for the standard deviation of the observed fraction, \\(\\sqrt{p(1-p)/N}\\), to be at most \\(0.01\\)? Someone measures a single qubit, prepared in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), a hundred times in a row, obtains \\(0\\) every time, and concludes that the state was \\(\\ket0\\). What do the data actually show? Design an experiment that distinguishes \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) from \\(\\ket0\\)."}, {"title": "Changes of basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-1", "text": "4.3.1 Changes of basis § Is the computational basis special? Consider \\[\\ket+=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2},\\qquad\\ket-=\\frac{\\ket0-\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Both are unit vectors, and \\(\\braket+-=\\tfrac12(1-1)=0\\), so they form another orthonormal basis. Every state can be written in this basis, and its coordinates are inner products: the coefficient of \\(\\ket+\\) in \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is \\(\\braket+\\psi\\). For example, \\(\\braket+0=\\braket-0=1/\\sqrt2\\), so \\[\\ket0=\\frac{\\ket++\\ket-}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Has anything physical changed? No. Writing \\(\\ket0\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis changes its coordinates, not the state. Applying a gate does change the state. The two can look alike on paper, so it matters to say which one is meant. The same holds for any orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\): \\(\\ket\\psi=\\sum_j\\braket{u_j}\\psi\\,\\ket{u_j}\\). These rows change coordinates without changing the physical state. The plus/minus basis is defined by \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Problems Write \\(\\ket1\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Write \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Let \\(\\ket{\\pm i}=(\\ket0\\pm i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Show that these two vectors form an orthonormal basis, and write \\(\\ket+\\) in it."}, {"title": "Measurement in an orthonormal basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-2", "text": "4.3.2 Measurement in an orthonormal basis § Apply \\(H\\) to a qubit and then measure it. Which property of the original state does the outcome reveal? \\(H\\) sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\). Write the original state as \\(a\\ket++b\\ket-\\); after \\(H\\) it is \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\). The measurement gives \\(0\\) with probability \\(|a|^2=|\\braket+\\psi|^2\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(|\\braket-\\psi|^2\\). “Apply \\(H\\), then measure” therefore measures the state in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Does this work for any orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\)? Let \\(U\\) be the unitary whose columns are the \\(\\ket{u_j}\\), so that \\(U\\ket j=\\ket{u_j}\\). Its inverse \\(U^\\dagger\\) sends \\(\\ket{u_j}\\) to \\(\\ket j\\). Applying \\(U^\\dagger\\) and then measuring gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\[|\\braket{j}{U^\\dagger\\psi}|^2=|\\braket{U j}\\psi|^2=|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2,\\] and applying \\(U\\) afterward turns the resulting state \\(\\ket j\\) into \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Measurement in a basis. Measuring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) in the orthonormal basis \\(\\{\\ket{u_j}\\}\\) gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2\\), and leaves the state \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Geometrically, \\(\\braket{u_j}\\psi\\,\\ket{u_j}\\) is the projection of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) onto the direction \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). With the projector \\(P_j=\\ket{u_j}\\bra{u_j}\\), the probability is \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\). More generally, a projective measurement is given by projectors \\(P_j\\) onto orthogonal subspaces with \\(\\sum_jP_j=I\\): outcome \\(j\\) has probability \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\) and leaves the state \\(P_j\\ket\\psi/\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|\\). When a subspace has more than one dimension, the outcome identifies the subspace, not a single vector. A real-coordinate illustration of the measurement rule. The projections are components used to calculate probabilities, not simultaneous fractional measurement results. Problems Measure \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Give the probability of each outcome. Let \\(\\ket{\\pm i}=(\\ket0\\pm i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Which operations, applied before a computational-basis measurement, measure a qubit in the basis \\(\\{\\ket{+i},\\ket{-i}\\}\\)? Check your answer on \\(\\ket{+i}\\). Let \\(U\\) be the unitary with columns \\(\\ket{u_j}\\). Show that applying \\(U^\\dagger\\) and then measuring in the computational basis gives outcome \\(j\\) with probability \\(|\\braket{u_j}\\psi|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Overlaps under unitary evolution", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-3", "text": "4.3.3 Overlaps under unitary evolution § What does a unitary preserve? For any two states, \\[\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\braket{\\phi}{U^\\dagger U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi\\psi.\\] Lengths are preserved, so total probability stays \\(1\\), and so are the overlaps between different states. Since measurement probabilities are squared overlaps, no operation can make two states more distinguishable than they already are. Could we allow any matrix and rescale the result to unit length afterward? No. Rescaling by a factor that depends on the state is not linear: it would break the sum rule, and the parts of a superposition would no longer evolve independently. Measurement, by contrast, does not preserve overlaps. The states \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) have overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), yet after the outcome \\(0\\) both are \\(\\ket0\\). Measurement is the one place where the description leaves unitary evolution. An inner product between two inputs equals that between their unitary outputs. Orthogonal inputs stay orthogonal; nonorthogonal inputs do not become perfectly distinguishable. Problems Check that \\(H\\) preserves the overlap of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\). The matrix \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) is not unitary. Find two states whose overlap it changes. Prove that a matrix that preserves the norm of every vector preserves every inner product. Expand \\(\\|\\phi+\\psi\\|^2\\) and \\(\\|\\phi+i\\psi\\|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Eigenvectors and eigenspaces", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-1", "text": "4.4.1 Eigenvectors and eigenspaces § To attach numbers to measurement outcomes, we need the states that an operator only rescales. \\(Z\\) leaves \\(\\ket0\\) alone and multiplies \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(-1\\). A vector \\(\\ket v\\neq0\\) with \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\) is an eigenvector of \\(A\\) with eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\): \\(A\\) keeps its direction and multiplies it by \\(\\lambda\\). For \\(Z\\), the vectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) are eigenvectors with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\). For \\(X\\), which exchanges \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\), we find \\(X\\ket+=\\ket+\\) and \\(X\\ket-=-\\ket-\\), with the same eigenvalues. What can the eigenvalues of a unitary be? A unitary preserves length, so \\(|\\lambda|=1\\). An eigenvector of a unitary only gains a phase. If several independent eigenvectors share an eigenvalue, every combination of them is again an eigenvector with that eigenvalue. Together they form an eigenspace . The outcome subspaces of a projective measurement will be the eigenspaces of the operator we are looking for. For \\(Z\\), the computational basis vectors are eigenvectors with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\). A general superposition need not be an eigenvector. Problems Show that \\(\\ket{+i}=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(\\ket{-i}=(\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) are eigenvectors of \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\), and find their eigenvalues. Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of \\(H\\). Prove that every eigenvalue of a unitary has magnitude \\(1\\), and that eigenvectors of a unitary with different eigenvalues are orthogonal."}, {"title": "Hermitian operators and spectral projections", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-2", "text": "4.4.2 Hermitian operators and spectral projections § We can now attach a number to each outcome. Choose a real number \\(a_j\\) for each outcome of a projective measurement and form \\[A=\\sum_ja_jP_j.\\] The eigenspaces of \\(A\\) are the outcome subspaces, and its eigenvalues are the reported numbers. Such a matrix satisfies \\(A^\\dagger=A\\); it is Hermitian . Conversely, every Hermitian matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors, so grouping equal eigenvalues writes it in the form \\(A=\\sum_ja_jP_j\\), its spectral decomposition . A Hermitian matrix used in this way is an observable , and measuring it gives \\(a_j\\) with probability \\(\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\). For example, \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,5)\\) has eigenvectors \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) with eigenvalues \\(2\\) and \\(5\\). Measuring \\(A\\) is a computational-basis measurement that reports \\(2\\) or \\(5\\). Two observables with the same eigenspaces but different eigenvalues ask the same question and report different numbers. To measure an observable is to specify both. For \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(2,5)\\), the projectors select the two computational-basis components. The measurement reports the associated eigenvalue. Problems Is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\-i&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) Hermitian? Find its eigenvalues. The observable \\(Z\\) reports \\(+1\\) for \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(-1\\) for \\(\\ket1\\). Find its average value, \\(\\sum_ja_jp_j\\), on \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\) and on \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Prove that the eigenvalues of a Hermitian matrix are real, and that measuring \\(A=\\sum_ja_jP_j\\) on \\(\\ket\\psi\\) has average value \\(\\braket{\\psi}{A\\psi}\\)."}, {"title": "Nonorthogonal state discrimination", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-3", "text": "4.4.3 Nonorthogonal state discrimination § Someone hands us one qubit, prepared either in \\(\\ket0\\) or in \\(\\ket+\\). Can we always tell which? Suppose a measurement could. Some outcome would then occur with certainty for \\(\\ket0\\) and never for \\(\\ket+\\). By the measurement postulate, \\(\\ket0\\) would lie in that outcome’s subspace and \\(\\ket+\\) would be orthogonal to it, so \\(\\braket0+=0\\). But \\(\\braket0+=1/\\sqrt2\\). No measurement identifies the two states with certainty. Could a cleverer procedure help, such as an operation before measuring, or an interaction that writes a record into another system? Any such operation is unitary and preserves the overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so the records cannot become orthogonal either. The limit is structural, not a weakness of detectors. Two pure states can be distinguished perfectly exactly when they are orthogonal. A measurement can trade wrong answers for answers that report “inconclusive,” but it cannot always be right. The real slice shown contains \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) at \\(45^\\circ\\). Their overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so one copy cannot always reveal which was prepared. Angles here represent state-vector geometry, not physical space. Problems Which of these pairs can be distinguished perfectly by one measurement: \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket1\\}\\); \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\); \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket+\\}\\); \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket{+i}\\}\\), where \\(\\ket{+i}=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)? A qubit is \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). We measure in the computational basis and guess \\(\\ket+\\) when the outcome is \\(1\\) and \\(\\ket0\\) when it is \\(0\\). Find the probability of guessing correctly. Prove that two pure states can be distinguished perfectly by a measurement exactly when they are orthogonal: give a measurement when they are, and show that none exists when they are not."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\(Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\) Hermitian, unitary, both, or neither? Answer the same for \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\). \\(Z\\) is both: \\(Z^\\dagger=Z\\) and \\(Z^\\dagger Z=Z^2=I\\). The second matrix is neither: its adjoint \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) differs from it, and \\(A^\\dagger A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}\\neq I\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-2", "text": "Normalize \\(3\\ket0+4i\\ket1\\). Find the probabilities of a measurement in the computational basis and in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. The normalized state is \\(\\tfrac35\\ket0+\\tfrac{4i}5\\ket1\\). Computational basis: \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\). \\(\\pm\\) basis: \\(\\braket\\pm\\psi=(3\\pm4i)/(5\\sqrt2)\\), each with squared magnitude \\(25/50=1/2\\). The relative phase \\(i\\) makes the \\(\\pm\\) outcomes equally likely, although the computational-basis probabilities are unequal."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-3", "text": "Prove that no unitary maps \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) to two orthogonal states. What does this imply for any procedure that tries to identify which of the two was prepared? \\(\\braket{U0}{U+}=\\braket0+=1/\\sqrt2\\neq0\\), so the outputs cannot be orthogonal. Any procedure built from operations followed by a measurement therefore cannot always identify the state: perfect identification would require orthogonal outputs."}, {"title": "Tensor products and product bases", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 Tensor products and product bases § A register of two qubits has four configurations, \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\), so its state has four amplitudes. Suppose we prepare the first qubit in \\(a\\ket0+b\\ket1\\) and, independently, the second in \\(c\\ket0+d\\ket1\\). What are the four joint amplitudes? Reaching the configuration \\(01\\) means that the first qubit is \\(0\\) and the second is \\(1\\). The two preparations are independent, so by the product rule the amplitude is the product of the two local amplitudes, \\(ad\\). Ordering the configurations with the first qubit as the leftmost bit, \\[\\begin{pmatrix}a\\\\b\\end{pmatrix}\\otimes\\begin{pmatrix}c\\\\d\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}ac\\\\ad\\\\bc\\\\bd\\end{pmatrix}.\\] This combination is the tensor product . For basis states it gives \\(\\ket a\\otimes\\ket b=\\ket{ab}\\); for example, \\(\\ket0\\otimes\\ket1=\\ket{01}\\). The four vectors \\(\\ket{ab}\\) form the product basis . Two qubits need four amplitudes and three qubits need eight: dimensions multiply rather than add. How does an operation on the first qubit alone act on the pair? It changes the first label and leaves the second unchanged: each arrow \\(i\\to j\\) of \\(U\\), with weight \\(w\\), becomes the arrows \\(ib\\to jb\\), with the same weight, for each value of \\(b\\). We write this operation \\(U\\otimes I\\). On a product state , \\((U\\otimes I)(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi)=(U\\ket\\psi)\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\). The joint basis has one label for every pair of local labels. This is the Cartesian arrangement of tensor-product basis vectors, not a mixture. Problems Compute \\(\\bigl(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2},\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}\\bigr)^T\\otimes(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\bigl(\\tfrac35,\\tfrac45\\bigr)^T\\otimes(0,1)^T\\). Compute \\((H\\otimes I)\\ket{01}\\) and \\((I\\otimes H)\\ket{01}\\). Show that \\(\\braket{a\\otimes b}{c\\otimes d}=\\braket ac\\braket bd\\) for vectors of two coordinates, and deduce that the tensor product of two unit vectors is a unit vector."}, {"title": "Product states", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Product states § Which joint states arise from independent preparations? A joint state of the form \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\) is a product state . Its amplitudes are products of local amplitudes, so its probabilities are products of local probabilities: the two qubits behave independently. Does a superposition of several joint configurations signal something more? Not necessarily. The state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) has two nonzero amplitudes, yet \\[\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0:\\] the first qubit is in an equal superposition, and the second is certainly \\(0\\). The real question is whether one local vector for each qubit reproduces every joint amplitude. When it does, the state is a product state. Both terms share the same second-qubit state. Factoring it out shows that this pure state is a product, despite having two nonzero amplitudes. Problems Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\) a product state? If so, factor it. Factor \\((\\ket{00}-\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})/2\\), and find the probability that the second qubit gives \\(0\\). Show that a two-qubit state with amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{00},\\alpha_{01},\\alpha_{10},\\alpha_{11}\\) is a product state exactly when \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}\\)."}, {"title": "The composition postulate", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 The composition postulate § The product rule tells us how independently prepared systems combine. Must every joint state be a product? Quantum mechanics does not require it. It takes the whole tensor-product space as the state space of the pair. Postulate 4 (composition). The state space of a composite system is the tensor product of the state spaces of its parts. Its basis vectors are the products of basis vectors of the parts. A register of \\(n\\) qubits therefore has \\(2^n\\) basis vectors, and a general state needs \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. Does a measurement reveal \\(2^n\\) numbers? No: measuring all \\(n\\) qubits gives a single \\(n\\)-bit label. Storing a general \\(n\\)-qubit state on a classical computer takes up to \\(2^n\\) amplitudes, which quickly becomes impractical. Does that by itself make quantum computers powerful? No. An advantage needs a task, and a procedure that makes its answer observable efficiently. The number of basis coefficients doubles with each added qubit. One computational-basis measurement still returns only one n-bit label. Problems How many amplitudes describe a general state of \\(10\\) qubits? How many bits does a measurement of all \\(10\\) qubits return? A classical simulation stores each amplitude as two \\(8\\)-byte numbers. How much memory does a general \\(40\\)-qubit state need? A product state of \\(n\\) qubits is described by \\(n\\) pairs of amplitudes. Show that applying one one-qubit operation to each qubit of a product state can be simulated with work proportional to \\(n\\). What does this show about the size of the state space as a source of difficulty?"}, {"title": "Bell-state preparation", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 Bell-state preparation § Start from \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit, then CNOT with the first qubit as control. What happens? \\(H\\) gives \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\), still a product state. CNOT acts on each configuration: it leaves \\(00\\) alone and sends \\(10\\) to \\(11\\): \\[\\ket{00}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The result is a Bell state , written \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). The circuit drawing records this sequence, with time running from left to right. Does the vertical line of CNOT mean that the first qubit is measured and the result used to decide whether to flip the second? No. CNOT acts on both configurations of the superposition at once, without any measurement. That is why the result is a superposition of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), rather than one of them. A Hadamard followed by CNOT prepares a Bell state. Read the circuit from left to right. Predict first. Reset to \\(\\ket{00}\\). After \\(H\\) on the first qubit, which two outcomes are possible? Which outcomes remain after CNOT? Experiment: build a Bell state Follow the joint state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) through a Hadamard on the first qubit and then CNOT. The four bars show computational-basis probabilities. Apply H to the first qubit Reset State: \\(\\ket{00}\\) Both qubits start in 0. The final pure state is entangled: its joint amplitudes do not factor into two single-qubit states. Its probabilities alone would not distinguish it from a classical mixture. After trying it: compare your explanation After \\(H\\), \\(00\\) and \\(10\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). CNOT changes \\(10\\) to \\(11\\), so the final outcomes are \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). The bars alone do not establish entanglement. Problems Apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit of \\(\\ket{10}\\), then CNOT with the first qubit as control. Which state results? Apply the same circuit to \\(\\ket{01}\\) and to \\(\\ket{11}\\). Show that the four states produced from \\(\\ket{00},\\ket{01},\\ket{10},\\ket{11}\\) form an orthonormal basis, and give a circuit that turns each of them back into its label."}, {"title": "Nonfactorization of amplitudes", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 Nonfactorization of amplitudes § Can \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) be written as a product \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\otimes(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\)? Compare amplitudes: we would need \\(ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(ad=bc=0\\). The first two equations make all four of \\(a,b,c,d\\) nonzero, and then \\(ad\\) cannot be \\(0\\). No product reproduces \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). A joint state that is not a product state is entangled . Neither qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) has a state vector of its own; only the pair does. Is entanglement just correlation? Measuring both qubits in the computational basis gives \\(00\\) or \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). A classical mixture, in which a coin decides whether to prepare \\(\\ket{00}\\) or \\(\\ket{11}\\), gives the same counts. The two differ in other measurements. A product \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\) would require \\(ac\\) and \\(bd\\) nonzero. Then all four factors are nonzero, contradicting \\(ad=bc=0\\). Problems Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})/2\\) entangled? For which real numbers \\(\\varepsilon\\) is \\((\\ket{00}+\\varepsilon\\ket{11})/\\sqrt{1+\\varepsilon^2}\\) a product state? For a two-qubit state in which both outcomes of the first qubit are possible, measure the first qubit in the computational basis. Show that the state is entangled exactly when the conditional state of the second qubit depends on the outcome."}, {"title": "Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-3", "text": "5.2.3 Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities § Neither qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) has a state of its own, yet each gives an outcome when measured. How do we read probabilities from a joint state? With amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{ab}\\), the joint probability is \\(p(a,b)=|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). The probability that the first qubit gives \\(a\\), whatever the second gives, is the marginal \\(p(a)=\\sum_bp(a,b)\\), and the conditional probability of \\(b\\) given \\(a\\) is \\(p(b\\mid a)=p(a,b)/p(a)\\) when \\(p(a)\\gt 0\\). For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\); each qubit alone gives \\(0\\) or \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\); and once the first result is known, the second is certain. Now measure both qubits in the \\(\\pm\\) basis. Rewriting \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) in that basis gives \\[\\ket{\\Phi^+}=\\frac{\\ket{++}+\\ket{--}}{\\sqrt2},\\] so the two results again agree with certainty. The classical mixture behaves differently. Each of \\(\\ket{00}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\) is a product of states that give \\(+\\) and \\(-\\) with probability \\(1/2\\), so in the \\(\\pm\\) basis the mixture gives all four pairs of results with probability \\(1/4\\). Correlation in one basis is therefore not enough to establish entanglement; the measurement must be specified. For the Bell state, the row sums give the first-qubit probabilities. Conditional on the first bit being zero, only the \\(00\\) entry remains possible. Problems For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), compute \\(p(\\text{first}=1)\\) and \\(p(\\text{second}=1\\mid\\text{first}=1)\\). For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt3\\), compute the joint probabilities, both marginal distributions, and \\(p(\\text{second}=1\\mid\\text{first}=0)\\). Both qubits of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) are measured in the basis \\(\\ket{u_0}=\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1\\), \\(\\ket{u_1}=-\\sin\\theta\\ket0+\\cos\\theta\\ket1\\). Show that the two results agree with certainty, for every \\(\\theta\\)."}, {"title": "Basis-state copying", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 Basis-state copying § Can a quantum state be copied? CNOT seems to copy. With a blank target, \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\to\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\to\\ket1\\ket1\\). What does it do to a superposition? By linearity, \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\to\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}.\\] Two copies would instead be \\[(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)=\\alpha^2\\ket{00}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{01}+\\alpha\\beta\\ket{10}+\\beta^2\\ket{11}.\\] These differ whenever \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are both nonzero. CNOT copies the labels \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), not superpositions of them. The first two rows copy computational-basis labels into a blank target. By linearity the third row produces an entangled state rather than two copies of \\(\\ket+\\). Problems Apply CNOT to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\) and to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket1\\). For which states \\(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) does CNOT turn \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\) into two copies, \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\otimes(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\)? A unitary \\(U\\) copies both basis states: \\(U\\ket0\\ket0=\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(U\\ket1\\ket0=\\ket1\\ket1\\). Show that, whatever \\(U\\) does on other inputs, it sends \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\ket0\\) to an entangled state."}, {"title": "The no-cloning theorem", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 The no-cloning theorem § Could some cleverer unitary copy every state? Suppose \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\) for every state \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Take two states \\(\\ket\\psi\\) and \\(\\ket\\phi\\). A unitary preserves inner products, and the inner product of two product states is the product of the inner products of their parts, so \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi\\braket00=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2,\\qquad\\text{that is,}\\qquad\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\braket\\phi\\psi^2.\\] The overlap must therefore be \\(0\\) or \\(1\\). For two different states that are not orthogonal, such as \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) with overlap \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), this fails. No unitary copies every state. This is the no-cloning theorem . What does the theorem not forbid? States known to belong to an orthogonal set, such as basis labels, can be copied, and a state that we know how to prepare can simply be prepared again. What is impossible is a single fixed operation that perfectly copies an arbitrary unknown state. Let \\(c=\\braket\\psi\\phi\\). A unitary cloning map would preserve input overlap \\(c\\), while two copies have overlap \\(c^2\\). The value \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) cannot satisfy that requirement. Problems Compute \\(\\braket0+\\) and \\(\\braket{00}{++}\\). Why do these values rule out a unitary that copies both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\)? Show that a unitary can copy each of the two states \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) into a register prepared in \\(\\ket0\\). Give one. A cloning machine may change its own state: \\(U\\ket\\psi\\ket0\\ket e=\\ket\\psi\\ket\\psi\\ket{e_\\psi}\\), where the final machine state \\(\\ket{e_\\psi}\\) may depend on \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Show that it can still clone two states only if they are orthogonal or equal up to phase."}, {"title": "Local states and no-signalling", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-3", "text": "5.3.3 Local states and no-signalling § Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), Alice holding the first qubit and Bob the second. What state does Bob’s qubit have on its own? Not a state vector, since the pair is entangled. Whatever basis Bob measures in, each of his two outcomes has probability \\(1/2\\). These predictions are summarized by the density matrix \\(I/2\\), described in Appendix D. Can Alice change Bob’s statistics by acting on her own qubit? Suppose she applies a unitary to it, or measures it without telling Bob the result. Bob’s outcome probabilities do not change. If they could, Alice could send a message instantly by choosing her action. When Alice tells Bob her result, his description does change: given her result \\(0\\), his qubit is \\(\\ket0\\). But that information travels through an ordinary classical channel. Entanglement provides correlations, not a way to signal. For the Bell pair, either local computational-basis distribution is fair. Learning the remote outcome permits conditioning; without it, the local distribution remains unchanged. Problems Alice and Bob share \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Alice measures her qubit and does not report the result. What is the probability that Bob obtains \\(0\\) in a computational-basis measurement? Alice applies \\(X\\) to her qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), without measuring. Find the new joint state and Bob’s computational-basis probabilities. Alice measures her qubit of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) in the \\(\\pm\\) basis instead. Find Bob’s conditional state for each of her results, and show that his computational-basis probabilities are still \\(1/2\\) each."}, {"title": "Entanglement resources", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-1", "text": "5.4.1 Entanglement resources § Alice holds a qubit in an unknown state \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) and wants Bob to have it. She cannot copy it, and measuring it would reveal only one bit. What can she do if they share a Bell pair? The three qubits start in \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\); Alice holds the first two and Bob the third. Alice applies CNOT from her input qubit to her half of the Bell pair, then \\(H\\) to her input qubit. Nothing quantum is sent to Bob. To see what has happened, we follow the complete three-qubit state. Alice and Bob first share a Bell pair. Alice’s joint measurement and two-bit message allow Bob to select the correction; the shared pair is consumed in transferring the input state. Problems In teleportation, how many qubits does each party hold at the start? Write the initial three-qubit state for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\). For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket1\\), apply Alice’s CNOT and \\(H\\), and group the result by the configuration of Alice’s two qubits. Suppose Alice sends Bob only the bit \\(m\\), and Bob applies \\(Z^m\\). Show that for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\) his qubit is wrong with probability \\(1/2\\)."}, {"title": "Measurement and conditional corrections", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-2", "text": "5.4.2 Measurement and conditional corrections § Start from \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). CNOT flips Alice’s Bell half where the input is \\(1\\), and \\(H\\) then acts on the input qubit: \\[\\frac{\\alpha\\ket0(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})+\\beta\\ket1(\\ket{10}+\\ket{01})}{\\sqrt2}\\;\\to\\;\\frac{\\alpha(\\ket0+\\ket1)(\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})+\\beta(\\ket0-\\ket1)(\\ket{10}+\\ket{01})}{2}.\\] Group the terms by the configuration of Alice’s two qubits: \\[\\tfrac12\\Bigl[\\ket{00}(\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)+\\ket{01}(\\alpha\\ket1+\\beta\\ket0)+\\ket{10}(\\alpha\\ket0-\\beta\\ket1)+\\ket{11}(\\alpha\\ket1-\\beta\\ket0)\\Bigr]=\\frac12\\sum_{m,n\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\ket{mn}\\otimes X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi.\\] Alice now measures her two qubits. Each outcome \\((m,n)\\) has probability \\(1/4\\) and leaves Bob’s qubit in \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\). Bob does not know which. If Alice sends him the two bits \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), he applies \\(X^n\\) and then \\(Z^m\\). Since \\(Z^mX^nX^nZ^m=I\\), his qubit ends in \\(\\ket\\psi\\). Does teleportation depend on a lucky outcome? No: the calculation covers all four outcomes, and each one is corrected. Alice’s two measured bits control Bob’s \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) corrections. Dashed lines carry classical information; the Bell pair is prepared at the left. Problems Alice obtains \\((m,n)=(1,0)\\). Which state does Bob hold, and which correction does he apply? For \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket+\\) and Alice’s result \\((1,1)\\), write Bob’s state before correction, and check that the correction restores \\(\\ket+\\). Bob applies his corrections in the wrong order: \\(Z^m\\) first, then \\(X^n\\). Show that he still obtains \\(\\ket\\psi\\), up to a global phase."}, {"title": "State transfer and classical communication", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-3", "text": "5.4.3 State transfer and classical communication § Could Bob use his qubit before the two bits arrive? Without them, his qubit is in each of the four states \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\) with probability \\(1/4\\). Averaged over the four, his predictions are those of a fair coin, \\(I/2\\), whatever \\(\\ket\\psi\\) is. He learns nothing about \\(\\ket\\psi\\), and nothing has travelled faster than the classical bits. Has the state been copied? No. Alice’s measurement leaves her qubits in \\(\\ket m\\ket n\\), with no trace of \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\), and the Bell pair has been used up. The unknown state now exists only at Bob, consistent with the no-cloning theorem. Teleportation places three descriptions side by side: the joint state of all three qubits, Bob’s state given Alice’s result, and Bob’s state when the result is unknown. Keeping them apart is the key to reasoning about entanglement. The two-bit message selects a correction using a previously shared Bell pair. It is not a classical list of the unknown amplitudes. Problems Before the classical bits arrive, what is the probability that Bob obtains \\(0\\) when measuring his qubit, for \\(\\ket\\psi=\\ket0\\)? Show that, before the bits arrive, Bob obtains \\(0\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) for every input \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\). Show that after Bob’s correction the three qubits are in the state \\(\\ket m\\ket n\\ket\\psi\\), and explain why this rules out a copy of \\(\\ket\\psi\\) anywhere else."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\) entangled? \\(\\alpha_{00}\\alpha_{11}=0\\) but \\(\\alpha_{01}\\alpha_{10}=1/3\\), so the state is not a product: it is entangled."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-2", "text": "Prepare \\(\\ket{\\Psi^-}=(\\ket{01}-\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) from \\(\\ket{00}\\) using \\(X\\), \\(H\\), and CNOT gates. Show that measuring both qubits in the same basis \\(\\{\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1,\\,-\\sin\\theta\\ket0+\\cos\\theta\\ket1\\}\\) always gives opposite results. \\(X\\otimes X\\) gives \\(\\ket{11}\\); \\(H\\) on the first qubit gives \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\); CNOT gives \\((\\ket{01}-\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). For real basis vectors, \\(\\braket{u_a\\otimes u_b}{\\Psi^-}=\\bigl(u_a(0)u_b(1)-u_a(1)u_b(0)\\bigr)/\\sqrt2\\), which is \\(0\\) when \\(a=b\\). So equal results never occur, in every such basis."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-3", "text": "Teleport \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). For each of Alice’s four outcomes \\((m,n)\\), give Bob’s state before correction and the correction he applies. \\((0,0)\\): \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), no correction. \\((0,1)\\): \\((i\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(X\\). \\((1,0)\\): \\((\\ket0-i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(Z\\). \\((1,1)\\): \\((-i\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), apply \\(X\\) and then \\(Z\\). In every case the result is \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Constant and balanced Boolean functions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Constant and balanced Boolean functions § Here is a small question about a function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\): is \\(f(0)\\) equal to \\(f(1)\\)? If they are equal, \\(f\\) is constant ; if they differ, \\(f\\) is balanced . For a one-bit input, every function is one or the other. What do we actually need to learn? One bit: equal or different. We do not need the values \\(f(0)\\) and \\(f(1)\\) themselves. Can an algorithm learn that one bit without learning both values? The gap between the answer we want and the full data is what the quantum algorithm will exploit. For inputs of more bits, a function need not be constant or balanced. Restricting attention to those two cases is then a promise about the input. Deutsch’s task asks whether the two outputs agree. It does not require identifying which truth table in a class was supplied. Problems List the four functions \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) as pairs \\((f(0),f(1))\\), and classify each as constant or balanced. Among the functions \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^2\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), how many are constant, how many are balanced (output \\(1\\) on exactly half the inputs), and how many are neither? For \\(f\\) on \\(n\\)-bit inputs, promised to be constant or balanced, show that a deterministic classical algorithm can decide which with \\(2^{n-1}+1\\) queries, and that \\(2^{n-1}\\) queries do not always suffice."}, {"title": "Classical query access", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Classical query access § Suppose the only access to \\(f\\) is a query : we choose \\(x\\) and receive \\(f(x)\\). How many queries does a classical algorithm need to answer with certainty? After one query, say with the reply \\(f(0)=0\\), both possibilities remain: \\(f(1)=0\\) makes \\(f\\) constant, and \\(f(1)=1\\) makes it balanced. One query cannot settle the question; two clearly suffice. This count belongs to the stated access model. If \\(f\\) were given in another form, for instance as a formula we could inspect, the comparison could change. If a classical query returns \\(f(0)=0\\), both a constant and a balanced truth table remain possible. A second value is needed for an exact classical answer. Problems A classical algorithm queries \\(f(1)\\) and receives \\(1\\). Which of the four functions remain possible? For \\(f\\) on two-bit inputs, promised constant or balanced, an algorithm queries \\(f(00)\\) and \\(f(01)\\). If they differ it answers “balanced”; otherwise it makes one more query. Complete the algorithm, and give its number of queries in the worst case. Show that no one-query classical algorithm for \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\), even one that chooses its query at random, is correct with probability greater than \\(1/2\\) on every function."}, {"title": "Reversible quantum oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Reversible quantum oracles § How can a quantum computation query \\(f\\)? A query must be a valid operation, hence unitary , while \\(f\\) itself may merge its inputs. The reversible embedding of a function solves this: keep the input and add \\(f(x)\\) to a target bit. \\[U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,\\,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] On the four configurations, \\(U_f\\) is a rearrangement: it exchanges \\((x,0)\\) and \\((x,1)\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\) and leaves them alone otherwise. Its matrix is a permutation matrix, so it is unitary, and applying it twice restores every configuration. What counts as one query? One use of \\(U_f\\), whatever state it is applied to, including a superposition . This is the black-box , or oracle , model: it counts the uses of \\(U_f\\) and ignores how \\(U_f\\) is built. The construction costs return when circuits are analysed in detail. For \\(f(x)=1\\), the two target values are swapped while \\(x\\) is kept. The full map is reversible even when the function \\(f\\) itself is not one-to-one. Problems Write the action of \\(U_f\\) on the four basis states for \\(f=(1,0)\\), that is, \\(f(0)=1\\) and \\(f(1)=0\\). Write the matrix of \\(U_f\\) for \\(f=(0,1)\\) in the order \\(00,01,10,11\\), and check that it is unitary. Show that the map \\(\\ket{x,y}\\mapsto\\ket{x,f(x)}\\), which overwrites the target, is not unitary for any \\(f\\), and that \\(\\ket x\\mapsto\\ket{f(x)}\\) is not unitary when \\(f\\) is constant."}, {"title": "Oracle action on superpositions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Oracle action on superpositions § What happens if we query \\(f\\) on both inputs at once? Prepare the input in \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and the target in \\(\\ket0\\). By linearity, \\(U_f\\) acts on each configuration separately: \\[U_f\\left(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\right)=\\frac{\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] Have we computed both values? Both appear in the state, but nothing has read them. Whether this helps depends on what we do next. With \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), a blank target becomes correlated with the input. Linearity determines the output on the entire superposition. Problems Apply \\(U_f\\) for \\(f=(1,1)\\) to \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\). For each of the four functions, write \\(U_f\\bigl(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\bigr)\\), and say which results are entangled. With \\(n\\) input qubits in the uniform superposition and a clean target, \\(U_f\\) produces \\(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\ket{f(x)}\\). Measuring both registers gives a uniformly random \\(x\\) with \\(f(x)\\). About how many runs are needed, on average, before every value \\(f(x)\\) has been seen?"}, {"title": "Register measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Register measurement § Suppose we measure both registers now. The result is one random pair \\((x,f(x))\\): we learn one value, not both. Repeating the experiment gives more samples, but that is no better than classical queries. To gain anything, the remaining steps must turn the property we want, equal or different, into states that a measurement can tell apart with certainty. For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), a computational-basis measurement samples one of these four labels. It does not output the full list of amplitudes. Problems Measure both registers of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). What are the possible results and their probabilities? How many function values does one run reveal? To decide constant or balanced, we repeatedly prepare \\((\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)})/\\sqrt2\\) and measure both registers until both inputs have been seen. How many runs does this take on average? Show that measuring only the input register of \\((\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)})/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each for all four functions."}, {"title": "Workspace entanglement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Workspace entanglement § Could we apply \\(H\\) to the input and measure, as in the two-step experiment with \\(H\\)? Look at the target. If \\(f(0)\\neq f(1)\\), the target holds different values in the two branches: it is a record of which input occurred. Branches that end in different configurations cannot cancel. Applying \\(H\\) to the input alone then gives a random outcome, just as when a separate bit records the path between two applications of \\(H\\). Is there a target state that the oracle leaves unchanged, so that no record forms, yet that still responds to \\(f\\)? Different workspace records can prevent the desired input interference. The shared-workspace example factors; the branch-record example does not. The common normalization factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) is omitted in the labels. Problems For \\(f=(0,1)\\), apply \\(H\\) to the input of \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), and find the probability of each input outcome. For the constant function \\(f=(0,0)\\), repeat the calculation: start from \\(\\tfrac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\), apply \\(U_f\\), then \\(H\\) to the input. Combining the two previous problems, design a one-query procedure with a clean target that never wrongly answers “balanced.” With what probability does it detect a balanced function?"}, {"title": "Bit-flip eigenstates", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Bit-flip eigenstates § The oracle either flips the target or leaves it alone. Which target states does a flip leave unchanged, apart from a factor? The eigenvectors of \\(X\\): \\[X\\ket+=\\ket+,\\qquad X\\ket-=-\\ket-,\\] since \\(X(\\ket0-\\ket1)=\\ket1-\\ket0\\). A flip therefore multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\), and no flip leaves it with the factor \\(+1\\): the target state stays the same while the factor records whether a flip occurred. The state \\(\\ket+\\) is unchanged in both cases and cannot tell them apart. The target we want is \\(\\ket-\\). A bit flip leaves \\(\\ket+\\) unchanged but multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\). Using the latter as the target makes a controlled flip supply a phase to the input branch. Problems Compute \\(X(\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Is \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) an eigenvector of \\(X\\)? Find every state that \\(X\\) leaves unchanged apart from a factor, by solving \\(X(a,b)^T=\\lambda(a,b)^T\\). The target is prepared in \\(\\cos\\theta\\ket0+\\sin\\theta\\ket1\\), with real \\(\\theta\\), and the oracle flips it. Show that its overlap with the original target is \\(\\sin2\\theta\\), and that the target keeps no record of the flip only when it is \\(\\ket+\\) or \\(\\ket-\\)."}, {"title": "Phase-oracle construction", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 Phase-oracle construction § Apply \\(U_f\\) to \\(\\ket x\\ket-\\). If \\(f(x)=0\\), nothing happens; if \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips, which multiplies the state by \\(-1\\): \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] With the input in \\(\\ket+\\), \\[U_f\\ket+\\ket-=\\frac{(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket-.\\] The target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) whatever \\(x\\) is, so it holds no record of the input. The function value has moved into the sign of the input amplitude . This is phase kickback . What do the signs mean? If \\(f\\) is constant, the two signs are equal and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket+\\); the common sign is a global phase . If \\(f\\) is balanced, the signs differ and the input is \\(\\pm\\ket-\\). The answer has become a relative phase . The target returns to the same minus state, while the input receives a function-dependent phase. Problems For \\(f=(1,0)\\), compute \\(U_f\\ket+\\ket-\\). For each of the four functions, give the input state after \\(U_f\\ket+\\ket-\\), up to a global phase. With \\(n\\) input qubits and the target in \\(\\ket-\\), show that \\(U_f\\bigl(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x\\ket x\\bigr)\\ket-=\\bigl(2^{-n/2}\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\bigr)\\ket-\\), and that the target ends in \\(\\ket-\\) for every \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Coherent uncomputation", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-3", "text": "6.3.3 Coherent uncomputation § What if \\(f\\) is not given as an oracle but computed by a circuit \\(A\\) that uses workspace, \\(A\\ket{x,0,0}=\\ket{x,f(x),g(x)}\\)? Apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, which multiplies the state by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\), then run \\(A\\) backward: \\[A^\\dagger Z_{\\mathrm{answer}}A\\ket{x,0,0}=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x,0,0}.\\] The workspace returns to \\(0\\), and the sign remains. This is compute, phase, uncompute . It is not free: it uses \\(A\\) and its inverse, and an honest count of the cost must include both. For a basis input \\(x\\), compute \\(f(x)\\) into clean workspace, apply \\(Z\\) to that bit, and reverse the computation. The function-dependent sign remains while the workspace returns to zero. Problems For \\(f(x)=x\\), computed into a clean answer bit by CNOT, compute \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes Z)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\) on \\(\\ket{0,0}\\) and \\(\\ket{1,0}\\). Compute \\(\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes Z)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\) on all four basis states, and identify the operation. Why does compute, phase, uncompute need the workspace to start at \\(0\\)? Omit the uncomputation: compute \\(f(x)=x\\) into the answer bit with CNOT and apply \\(Z\\) to the answer bit, but do not run CNOT again. Starting from \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), apply \\(H\\) to the input at the end. Show that the outcome is random, whereas with uncomputation it is certain."}, {"title": "Interference and readout", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Interference and readout § After the query, the input is \\((s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), with \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\). How do we read the relative sign? Apply \\(H\\), which sends \\(\\ket+\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to \\(\\ket1\\): \\[H\\,\\frac{s_0\\ket0+s_1\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{s_0+s_1}2\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}2\\ket1.\\] Equal signs give \\(0\\) with certainty; opposite signs give \\(1\\) with certainty. In terms of paths, the two contributions to the wrong answer cancel. The outcome depends only on whether \\(s_0\\) and \\(s_1\\) agree, never on \\(f(0)\\) or \\(f(1)\\) separately. We learn the one bit we wanted, and nothing more. Ignoring a common global sign, constant functions leave the input in \\(\\ket+\\) and balanced functions produce \\(\\ket-\\). The final \\(H\\) converts this distinction into a definite basis outcome. Problems Compute \\(H\\bigl((-\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\bigr)\\). For each of the four functions, compute the final input amplitudes \\((s_0+s_1)/2\\) and \\((s_0-s_1)/2\\). Show that if the final \\(H\\) is omitted, or replaced by \\(X\\), the measurement gives \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) with probability \\(1/2\\) each, for every function."}, {"title": "Quantum and classical query counts", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Quantum and classical query counts § Put the steps together. Prepare \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\) and apply \\(H\\) to each qubit , giving \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\). Query \\(U_f\\) once. Apply \\(H\\) to the input, and measure it. This is Deutsch’s algorithm . It decides whether \\(f\\) is constant or balanced with one query and with certainty, where any classical method needs two. Is the comparison fair? Both use the same oracle and demand the same certainty. The other gates are real operations, but they are not queries. A query separation is a first example of quantum advantage , not a statement about total running time on real hardware. Deutsch’s algorithm: prepare, query, recombine, and measure. Only the joint U_f box counts as an oracle query. Predict first. Compare a constant function with a balanced function. Before running the circuit, predict whether the final input bit is 0 or 1. Would complementing every function value change that bit? Experiment: an oracle changes relative phase Prepare the input in \\(\\ket{+}\\) and the target in \\(\\ket{-}\\). The oracle leaves the target unchanged and multiplies each input amplitude by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). A final Hadamard makes the relative sign observable. Function f(0) = 0, f(1) = 0 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 0 Apply oracle Reset |0⟩ +1/√2 |1⟩ +1/√2 Both input amplitudes have the same sign. Choose a function, then apply its oracle. A common sign changes no probabilities. Opposite signs change the result after the final Hadamard. After trying it: compare your explanation A constant function gives 0; a balanced function gives 1. Complementing the function multiplies the state after the phase oracle by a common minus sign, so it does not change the final measurement. Problems Count the queries and the other operations in Deutsch’s algorithm, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\). Run Deutsch’s algorithm for \\(f=(1,0)\\), giving the state after each step. With \\(n\\) input qubits, after the query and \\(H\\) applied to each input qubit, show that the amplitude of the input configuration \\(0^n\\) is \\(2^{-n}\\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}\\). Deduce that one query distinguishes constant from balanced functions with certainty."}, {"title": "Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement § What made the algorithm work? Four stages: prepare coherent alternatives; encode the property we want in their relative phases; recombine them; and measure in a basis that exposes the difference. Each stage matters. Without the \\(\\ket-\\) target, a record would block the interference ; without the final \\(H\\), the signs would stay invisible. A short circuit can still fail if the final states for different answers cannot be told apart, so correctness requires following the state through all four stages, workspace included. Searching for a marked input starts from the same kind of phase mark, but needs repeated recombination to turn the mark into a likely answer. The target \\(\\ket-\\) lets the oracle encode a phase without keeping an input-dependent target record. The final \\(H\\) converts that phase into the answer. Problems Name the four stages of Deutsch’s algorithm and the operations used in each. Replace the target \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(\\ket+\\), and run the algorithm for \\(f=(0,1)\\). What does the measurement give? Leave out the first \\(H\\) on the input, so that the input stays \\(\\ket0\\). Show that the algorithm then gives each outcome with probability \\(1/2\\) for every function."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(f=(0,1)\\), what does \\(U_f\\) do to \\(\\ket1\\ket-\\)? The target flips, which multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\): \\(U_f\\ket1\\ket-=-\\ket1\\ket-\\). The function value appears as a sign, and the target is unchanged."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run the \\(n\\)-qubit version of Deutsch’s algorithm for \\(n=2\\) and the balanced function \\(f(x_1x_2)=x_1\\). Show that the outcome \\(00\\) never occurs. After the query, the input is \\(\\tfrac12(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}-\\ket{10}-\\ket{11})=\\ket-\\ket+\\). Applying \\(H\\otimes H\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\), so the outcome is \\(10\\), never \\(00\\). The function is identified as balanced with one query."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-3", "text": "Show that Deutsch’s algorithm gives the same outcome for \\(f\\) and for its complement \\(1-f\\), and that the outcome depends only on \\(f(0)\\oplus f(1)\\). For \\(1-f\\), each sign \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\) is multiplied by \\(-1\\), a global phase that changes no probability. The outcome is \\(1\\) exactly when \\(s_0\\neq s_1\\), that is, when \\(f(0)\\oplus f(1)=1\\). The algorithm extracts this one bit and nothing more about \\(f\\)."}, {"title": "Search predicates and classical queries", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Search predicates and classical queries § Among \\(N\\) labels, some are marked, and a predicate \\(f\\) tells us whether a label \\(x\\) is marked: \\(f(x)=1\\) if it is, and \\(0\\) otherwise. We want to find a marked label. How many queries does a classical search need? With one marked label in an unknown position, each query tests one candidate. Checking labels one by one may take \\(N-1\\) queries in the worst case, and about \\(N/2\\) on average. To succeed with a fixed probability, a classical search needs a number of queries proportional to \\(N\\). We start with \\(N=4\\) and mark the label \\(11\\). As before, we count queries to \\(f\\) and keep the other operations in view. The task is to output a marked label, not to estimate how many there are. In this four-item example only \\(11\\) is marked. Testing a supplied candidate is different from locating the successful one. Problems A predicate on three-bit labels marks the labels whose bits add to \\(2\\). How many labels are marked, and what is the probability that a uniformly random label is marked? One label among \\(N=8\\) is marked. A classical search queries labels in a uniformly random order, without repetition, until it finds the marked one. What is the expected number of queries? One label among \\(N\\) is marked, in a uniformly random position. Show that any classical algorithm making \\(k\\) queries finds it with probability at most \\(k/N\\)."}, {"title": "State preparation and phase marking", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 State preparation and phase marking § Can a quantum search do better than testing candidates one at a time? It can begin with all candidates at once. Two Hadamards prepare the uniform superposition \\[\\ket s=\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11}}2.\\] How should a query act? Phase kickback turns a query into a sign: \\[O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x.\\] With \\(11\\) marked, the amplitudes become \\((1,1,1,-1)/2\\). Has this helped? Not yet. Every probability is still \\(1/4\\), and measuring now finds \\(11\\) with probability \\(1/4\\), as a guess would. The query has created a relative sign, and a further operation must turn that sign into probability. The phase oracle \\(O_f\\) may be supplied directly, or built by phase kickback or by compute, phase, uncompute. Its cost in queries must be stated accordingly. The marked item is \\(11\\). Every immediate measurement probability stays \\(1/4\\), so another coherent operation is needed to turn the sign into an advantage. Problems For \\(N=4\\) with the label \\(01\\) marked, write the amplitudes after preparing the uniform superposition and applying the phase oracle. For \\(N=8\\) with one marked label, what are the amplitudes after preparation and marking, and what is the probability of measuring the marked label? Show that applying the phase oracle any number of times, with nothing in between, never changes any measurement probability."}, {"title": "Reflection and final amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Reflection and final amplitudes § Which operation turns a negative sign into a larger amplitude ? Look at the amplitudes \\((1/2,1/2,1/2,-1/2)\\). Their mean is \\(\\mu=1/4\\). The marked amplitude lies far below the mean, and the others slightly above it. Reflect every amplitude about the mean, \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\): \\[\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14-\\tfrac12=0,\\qquad-\\tfrac12\\mapsto2\\cdot\\tfrac14+\\tfrac12=1.\\] One marking and one reflection find \\(11\\) with certainty. Is reflection about the mean a valid quantum operation? It is the matrix \\[D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I.\\] Indeed, \\(\\braket s a=\\sum_xa_x/\\sqrt N\\), and every entry of \\(\\ket s\\) is \\(1/\\sqrt N\\), so the entries of \\(2\\ket s\\braket sa\\) are \\(2\\mu\\), and \\(D\\ket a\\) has entries \\(2\\mu-a_x\\). Since \\(D^2=4\\ket s\\bra s-4\\ket s\\bra s+I=I\\) and \\(D^\\dagger=D\\), it is unitary . It does not estimate the mean from samples; it acts on the amplitudes directly. A complete two-qubit search for 11. The first controlled-Z marks 11. The remaining coherent gates implement diffusion up to global phase. Both measured bits are one. Problems Reflect the amplitudes \\(\\tfrac12(1,-1,1,1)\\) about their mean. For \\(N=8\\) with one marked label, compute the amplitudes after one marking and one reflection about the mean, and the success probability. Show directly from \\(a_x\\mapsto2\\mu-a_x\\) that reflection about the mean preserves the sum of squared amplitudes, for real amplitudes."}, {"title": "Good and bad subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 Good and bad subspaces § With \\(N\\) labels and \\(M\\) of them marked, \\(0\\lt M\\lt N\\), what stays simple as \\(N\\) grows? In the uniform start, and after every marking and reflection, all marked labels have equal amplitudes, and so do all unmarked ones. So group them. Let \\(\\ket G\\) be the normalized equal superposition of the marked labels, and \\(\\ket B\\) that of the unmarked labels. They are orthogonal, because no label belongs to both. The uniform state lies in their plane: \\[\\ket s=\\sqrt{M/N}\\,\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-M/N}\\,\\ket B.\\] We never need to construct \\(\\ket G\\) or \\(\\ket B\\) explicitly. They describe the state, and they let us prove what the operations do. For one marked item among four, \\(\\ket{G}=\\ket{11}\\) and \\(\\ket{B}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\). These orthogonal directions contain the initial state and the entire ideal search trajectory. Problems For \\(N=8\\) with the marked labels \\(011\\) and \\(101\\), write \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). For \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=2\\), write \\(\\ket s\\) as a combination of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). Show that the oracle \\(O_f\\) and the reflection \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) both map the plane of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\) into itself, by computing their action on \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\)."}, {"title": "Initial success amplitude", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 Initial success amplitude § Where does \\(\\ket s\\) lie in this plane? Write \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}\\), with \\(0\\lt\\theta\\lt\\pi/2\\). Then \\[\\ket s=\\sin\\theta\\,\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\,\\ket B:\\] the state makes the angle \\(\\theta\\) with the bad direction, and its success probability is \\(\\sin^2\\theta=M/N\\). For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), or \\(30^\\circ\\). Why is one angle enough, however many labels the register has? Both the oracle and the reflection map the plane to itself, so the state never leaves it. With one marked item among four, \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\). The good amplitude is the vertical component; the success probability is its square. Problems Find the angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(N=16\\) with \\(M=1\\), and for \\(N=8\\) with \\(M=2\\). For \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=2\\), how many iterations give success with certainty? Show that, for \\(0\\lt M\\lt N\\), exactly one Grover iteration gives certain success precisely when \\(M/N=1/4\\)."}, {"title": "Reflections and rotations", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Reflections and rotations § What does the oracle do in this plane? It negates \\(\\ket G\\) and keeps \\(\\ket B\\): a reflection across the bad axis. The operation \\(D\\) is a reflection across \\(\\ket s\\). Two reflections in a plane make a rotation, by twice the angle between their axes. Here the axes are the bad direction and \\(\\ket s\\), at the angle \\(\\theta\\), so each Grover iteration \\(DO_f\\) rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\) toward the good direction. For \\(N=4\\), the state starts at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\), and the reflection across \\(\\ket s\\) sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\), the good direction: the certain success found above. The picture also predicts what happens if we continue. The rotation does not stop at the good direction; it carries the state past it. For \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\), the initial state is at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\) about the bad axis; reflection about the initial direction then sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\). These are real coordinates in the good/bad plane. Problems For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), by how many degrees does each Grover iteration rotate the state? For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), give the angle of the state and the success probability after \\(1\\), \\(2\\), \\(3\\), and \\(4\\) iterations. In the plane with the bad direction as the first axis and the good direction as the second, reflection across the line at angle \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos2\\alpha&\\sin2\\alpha\\\\\\sin2\\alpha&-\\cos2\\alpha\\end{pmatrix}\\). Verify that reflecting across the bad axis and then across \\(\\ket s\\) is a rotation by \\(2\\theta\\)."}, {"title": "Amplitude evolution", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Amplitude evolution § Where is the state after \\(k\\) iterations, and how likely is success? It starts at the angle \\(\\theta\\) and turns by \\(2\\theta\\) each time, so it reaches \\((2k+1)\\theta\\): \\[\\ket{\\psi_k}=\\sin\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket G+\\cos\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\ket B,\\qquad p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr).\\] Which marked label do we find? All marked labels share the amplitude of \\(\\ket G\\), so given success, the result is uniform over the marked set. The success probability depends on the marked fraction \\(M/N\\), not on which labels are marked. For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(p_k=\\sin^2((2k+1)\\pi/6)\\). One iteration is ideal, but the next two return to probability \\(1/4\\). Stopping is part of the algorithm. Problems For \\(N=16\\) and \\(M=1\\), compute \\(p_k=\\sin^2\\bigl((2k+1)\\theta\\bigr)\\) for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=3\\). For \\(N=64\\) and \\(M=1\\), which number of iterations maximizes the success probability, and what is that probability? Show that, given success, the measured label is uniformly distributed over the \\(M\\) marked labels, and that, given failure, it is uniform over the unmarked labels."}, {"title": "Iteration count and overshooting", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Iteration count and overshooting § How many iterations should we perform? Success is greatest when \\((2k+1)\\theta\\) is close to \\(\\pi/2\\), that is, for \\(k\\) close to \\(\\pi/(4\\theta)-1/2\\). For a small marked fraction \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{M/N}\\), so the number of iterations is about \\(\\tfrac\\pi4\\sqrt{N/M}\\): of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\), where a classical search needs of order \\(N/M\\) queries. Since \\(k\\) must be an integer, success is usually close to \\(1\\) but not exactly \\(1\\); \\(N=4\\) with \\(M=1\\) is a special case in which it is exact. Does iterating longer help? No. For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), the success probabilities for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are \\(1/4\\), \\(1\\), and \\(1/4\\): the second iteration rotates the state past the good direction. The stopping rule is part of the algorithm . Predict first. Set Search space to 4 and reset. There is one marked item; start from equal amplitudes. Predict the success probability after one iteration and after a second. Does another iteration always help? Experiment: rotate toward the marked item There is one marked item. A full Grover iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). Keep stepping after the first peak to see why overshooting matters. Search space 4 16 64 256 One iteration Play Reset Iterations 0 unmarked component → marked component ↑ Probability of finding the marked item: 6.25%. The initial state spreads probability uniformly over all items. After trying it: compare your explanation One iteration gives success probability 1. A second gives \\(1/4\\): continued rotation can move the state away from the marked direction. Compare this with the initial probability \\(1/4\\). Problems For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), compute the success probability after three iterations. For \\(N=2^{20}\\approx10^6\\) and \\(M=1\\), estimate the number of Grover iterations, and compare it with the expected number of classical queries. Show that the sequence of success probabilities \\(p_0,p_1,p_2,\\dots\\) is periodic when \\(\\theta\\) is a rational multiple of \\(\\pi\\), and find the period for \\(N=4\\), \\(M=1\\)."}, {"title": "Unknown marked fraction and verification", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Unknown marked fraction and verification § What if we do not know \\(M\\), and so cannot compute the stopping point? A fixed guess may overshoot. A standard approach chooses a random number of iterations from a range that grows gradually, measures, and checks the candidate with one query to \\(f\\). When \\(M\\gt 0\\), such schedules still need an expected number of queries of order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\). What if nothing is marked? Then no check ever succeeds, and an unbounded search never ends; it cannot prove that nothing is marked. A bounded version stops after a fixed budget and reports failure, with a stated probability of being wrong when marked labels do exist. That is a statistical guarantee, not a proof of absence. If every label is marked, the very first measurement succeeds. Further detail: Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, and Tapp: Tight bounds on quantum searching . A failed verification rejects that candidate, not every possible candidate. With an unknown marked fraction, the schedule must choose how long to search before measuring again. Problems An algorithm succeeds with probability \\(p\\) per attempt and checks its candidate with one query. How many attempts does it need on average to find a marked label? For \\(N=1024\\), the iteration count tuned for \\(M=1\\) is \\(k=25\\). What is the success probability if in fact \\(M=4\\)? Each verified attempt succeeds with probability at least \\(1/4\\), independently. Show that \\(t\\) attempts all fail with probability at most \\((3/4)^t\\), and find the smallest \\(t\\) for which this is below \\(10^{-6}\\)."}, {"title": "Arbitrary state preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Arbitrary state preparation § Was the uniform start essential? Suppose a unitary \\(A\\) prepares \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and \\(P_G\\) projects onto the outcomes we count as successes, with success probability \\(p=\\|P_G\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\), where \\(0\\lt p\\lt 1\\). Normalizing the good and bad parts writes \\[\\ket\\psi=\\sqrt p\\,\\ket G+\\sqrt{1-p}\\,\\ket B.\\] This is the same plane as before, with \\(\\sin^2\\theta=p\\). Where might \\(A\\) come from? A classical randomized procedure can sometimes be made coherent and serve as \\(A\\), but only through an explicit reversible implementation, including its random bits and its workspace. Access to a routine that produces samples is not enough. A nonuniform preparation can still be resolved into normalized good and bad components. This example has \\(p=1/4\\), so its amplitudes are \\(1/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt3/2\\) regardless of how many underlying labels contribute. Problems A preparation succeeds with probability \\(p=1/9\\). Find \\(\\theta\\) and the amplitudes of \\(\\ket G\\) and \\(\\ket B\\). For \\(p=1/9\\), how many amplification iterations maximize success, and what is the success probability? A one-qubit preparation \\(A\\) gives \\(A\\ket0=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\ket0+\\tfrac12\\ket1\\), and success means outcome \\(1\\). Show that one amplification iteration succeeds with certainty, even though this preparation is not uniform."}, {"title": "Reflection using preparation and its inverse", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Reflection using preparation and its inverse § The search reflected about \\(\\ket s\\). How do we reflect about \\(\\ket\\psi\\) without knowing it? Reflection about \\(\\ket0\\), \\(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\), is easy: it negates every configuration except \\(0\\). Bring \\(\\ket\\psi\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) with \\(A^\\dagger\\), reflect there, and return with \\(A\\): \\[R_\\psi=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I=A\\bigl(2\\ket0\\bra0-I\\bigr)A^\\dagger.\\] Applied to a state, the rightmost factor \\(A^\\dagger\\) acts first. Together with \\(I-2P_G\\), which negates the successful part, \\(R_\\psi\\) rotates the state toward the good direction exactly as in search. What does this require? Coherent access to \\(A\\), to its inverse, and to the success test. Measuring whether a sample succeeded is no substitute: it destroys the superposition instead of reflecting it. The inverse preparation acts first. Conjugating the zero-state reflection produces reflection about the prepared state. Problems For \\(A=H\\) on one qubit, write \\(R_\\psi=A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger\\) as a matrix. Show that \\(A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I\\) when \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and that it is unitary. Why can the reflection \\(R_\\psi\\) not be replaced by measuring whether the prepared state succeeded? Show that measuring after each oracle step makes the success probability stay at \\(p\\)."}, {"title": "Success probability and resource cost", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-3", "text": "7.4.3 Success probability and resource cost § What do we gain? With independent trials, each succeeding with probability \\(p\\), we need about \\(1/p\\) preparations for a fixed chance of success. Amplitude amplification needs about \\(1/\\sqrt p\\) iterations, each using \\(A\\), \\(A^\\dagger\\), and the success test once. If \\(A\\) costs \\(C_A\\) and the test costs \\(C_G\\), the total is of order \\((C_A+C_G)/\\sqrt p\\), plus the cost of verifying the answer and of choosing the number of iterations. The improvement is quadratic in the number of uses of these resources. If preparing the state or building the oracle is itself expensive, that cost can dominate the total. For small \\(p\\), the improvement concerns repeated use of coherent preparation and the success test. Their implementation costs must be included. Problems For \\(p=0.01\\), compare the number of independent trials needed for a reasonable chance of success with the number of amplification iterations. Preparing \\(A\\) costs \\(100\\) gates and the success test \\(20\\) gates. For \\(p=10^{-4}\\), compare the total cost of about \\(1/p\\) independent trials, each a preparation and a test, with about \\(\\tfrac\\pi4/\\sqrt p\\) amplification iterations, each using \\(A\\), \\(A^\\dagger\\), and the test. Preparing an arbitrary list of \\(N\\) amplitudes costs about \\(N\\) gates. For a search among \\(N\\) items with one marked item, so that \\(p=1/N\\), compare the cost of amplitude amplification with a classical scan of the list."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-1", "text": "For \\(N=32\\) and \\(M=2\\), find \\(\\sin\\theta\\) and the initial success probability. \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{2/32}=1/4\\), and the initial success probability is \\(1/16\\), the same as for one marked item among sixteen."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-2", "text": "For \\(N=32\\) and \\(M=2\\), find the number of Grover iterations that maximizes success, and the success probability. With \\(\\theta\\approx14.48^\\circ\\), the success probabilities for \\(k=2,3,4\\) are about \\(0.91\\), \\(0.96\\), and \\(0.58\\). The best is \\(k=3\\), with success about \\(0.96\\). Only the marked fraction matters, not \\(N\\) or \\(M\\) separately."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-3", "text": "For \\(N=4\\) with two marked items, show that Grover iterations never change the success probability. \\(\\theta=45^\\circ\\), so the angles \\((2k+1)45^\\circ\\) are odd multiples of \\(45^\\circ\\), and \\(\\sin^2\\) of every one is \\(1/2\\). Iterating never helps: when half the items are marked, a random guess is already as good as the algorithm can do."}, {"title": "Wires and gate ordering", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 Wires and gate ordering § How can we write down a long sequence of operations on many qubits without writing out their matrices? Draw one horizontal line, a wire , for each qubit , with time running from left to right, and a box on a wire for each operation applied to that qubit. This is a circuit diagram . What does a wire carry? Not a definite bit. When the register is in a superposition or an entangled state, no single wire has a value of its own. To read a circuit, follow the joint state of the whole register from one column of gates to the next. In what order are the matrices multiplied? If \\(U\\) acts first and \\(V\\) second, the final state is \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\): the drawing reads from left to right, and the product from right to left. Does the order of gates on different wires matter? No. Gates on disjoint wires act as tensor products, such as \\(U\\otimes I\\) and \\(I\\otimes V\\), and these commute. A real device may still have to schedule them in a particular way. The circuit applies \\(H\\) first and \\(Z\\) second. On a column vector the rightmost matrix acts first, so the combined operator is \\(ZH\\). Problems A circuit applies \\(H\\) to qubit 1, then \\(X\\) to qubit 2, then CNOT with qubit 1 as control and qubit 2 as target, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\). Write the state after each step. Apply, to \\(\\ket{00}\\), first \\(H\\) on qubit 1 and then CNOT, and in the other order. Do the two operations commute? Prove that \\((U\\otimes I)(I\\otimes V)=(I\\otimes V)(U\\otimes I)=U\\otimes V\\): gates on different wires commute."}, {"title": "Circuits for query and search algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Circuits for query and search algorithms § Read the algorithms we have met as circuits. In Deutsch’s circuit, the first pair of \\(H\\) gates prepares \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), the oracle box puts the function into signs, and the last \\(H\\) exposes their relation. In search, a block consisting of the phase oracle and the reflection \\(D\\) is repeated. Does a box labelled \\(O_f\\) tell us how to build \\(O_f\\)? No. Drawing an operation as a single box does not make it a single elementary gate; its cost is unknown until we say how it is implemented. A query diagram counts uses of an oracle. Replacing each box by a circuit exposes the gate cost, plus any operations between calls. Problems In the four-item search circuit, how many oracle uses and how many Hadamard gates does one Grover iteration contain, and how many Hadamard gates prepare the start? List the gates of Deutsch’s algorithm in time order, starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\), with the qubits each acts on. An oracle box \\(O_f\\) on \\(n\\) qubits marks one fixed string. It is built from \\(X\\) gates and a phase flip controlled on all \\(n\\) qubits, which in turn uses Toffoli gates and \\(n-2\\) workspace qubits. Count the gates in such a construction."}, {"title": "Measurement and classical control", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Measurement and classical control § How does a circuit show a measurement? A meter symbol ends the quantum description of that wire and produces a classical bit. In our drawings, solid lines carry qubits and dashed lines carry classical bits. Is a gate controlled by a measured bit the same as a controlled gate? No. A classically controlled gate is chosen using a known result. A coherent controlled gate acts on an unmeasured control that may be in a superposition, and it can create entanglement . Teleportation uses both: a coherent CNOT before Alice’s measurement, and classically controlled corrections afterward. Classical control uses a recorded outcome. It selects a branch after measurement rather than preserving a coherent superposition of both control alternatives. Problems Bob receives a bit \\(b\\) and applies \\(X\\) only if \\(b=1\\). Write his operation for each value of \\(b\\). For the input \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)\\ket0\\), compare (a) CNOT followed by measuring the control with (b) measuring the control first and then applying \\(X\\) to the target if the result is \\(1\\). Show that the outcomes and final states agree. Now the control is not measured at the end. Compare CNOT with “measure the control, then apply \\(X\\) if \\(1\\)” on the input \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), by applying \\(H\\) to the control afterward and measuring it."}, {"title": "Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates § Which one-qubit gates do we need? We have met three: \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] A fourth, \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\), completes the Pauli matrices \\(I\\), \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\). Fixed gates turn a qubit by fixed amounts. A general operation, such as the half of a NOT met earlier, needs turns of any size. For a Pauli matrix \\(P\\), define the rotation gate \\[R_P(\\theta)=\\cos\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I-i\\sin\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P.\\] Is it unitary ? Since \\(P^\\dagger=P\\) and \\(P^2=I\\), we get \\(R_P(\\theta)^\\dagger R_P(\\theta)=\\cos^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,I+\\sin^2\\tfrac\\theta2\\,P^2=I\\). It is. Two fixed rotations will matter when gates must come from a finite set: the phase gates \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\). They change the relative phase of \\(\\ket1\\) by \\(\\pi/2\\) and by \\(\\pi/4\\). Predict first. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\), compare applying \\(H\\) twice with applying \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Predict both final measurement distributions before trying the gates. Experiment: a gate changes amplitudes Start in \\(\\ket{0}\\). Apply \\(H\\) twice, then compare \\(HZH\\) with a single \\(X\\). Orange marks a negative amplitude; the bar length shows its squared magnitude. Apply H Apply X Apply Z Reset to \\(\\ket0\\) State: \\(1\\ket0+0\\ket1\\) Choose a gate to change the state. This experiment uses real amplitudes. It illustrates X, Z, and H, not every possible qubit state. After trying it: compare your explanation \\(H\\) followed by \\(H\\) returns \\(\\ket0\\), so 0 is certain. \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) produces \\(\\ket1\\), so 1 is certain. \\(Z\\) changes a relative sign that the final \\(H\\) converts into a different outcome. \\(X\\) exchanges basis labels; \\(Z\\) changes one sign; \\(H\\) changes basis. Their action on arbitrary superpositions follows by linearity. Problems Compute \\(R_X(\\pi)\\) and \\(R_Z(\\pi)\\), and compare them with \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\). Verify by multiplying matrices that \\(HZH=X\\), \\(HXH=Z\\), \\(S^2=Z\\), and \\(T^2=S\\). Show that \\(R_Z(\\theta)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-i\\theta/2},e^{i\\theta/2})\\), and that up to a global phase it is \\(\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\theta})\\). Which values of \\(\\theta\\) give \\(S\\) and \\(T\\)?"}, {"title": "Controlled and entangling gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Controlled and entangling gates § How do we make a gate act on a target only when a control qubit is \\(1\\)? Use the projectors onto the two values of the control: \\[\\text{controlled-}U=\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U.\\] It keeps the control and applies \\(U\\) to the target in the branch where the control is \\(1\\). CNOT is controlled-\\(X\\). Controlled-\\(Z\\) multiplies only \\(\\ket{11}\\) by \\(-1\\). Can one be made from the other? Since \\(HXH=Z\\), applying \\(H\\) to the target before and after a CNOT gives controlled-\\(Z\\). Do controlled gates always entangle? Not on every input: CNOT leaves \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) a product state . On \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\), however, it produces a Bell state . Hadamards on the target convert controlled-X to controlled-Z. Problems Apply controlled-\\(Z\\) to the four basis states. Show that exchanging the roles of control and target gives the same operation. Verify that \\((I\\otimes H)\\,\\mathrm{CNOT}\\,(I\\otimes H)\\) acts as controlled-\\(Z\\) on \\(\\ket{10}\\) and \\(\\ket{11}\\). Let \\(\\ket v\\) be an eigenvector of \\(U\\) with \\(U\\ket v=e^{i\\phi}\\ket v\\). Show that controlled-\\(U\\) sends \\(\\ket+\\ket v\\) to \\(\\tfrac1{\\sqrt2}(\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)\\otimes\\ket v\\). Is the result entangled?"}, {"title": "Oracle and reflection circuits", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Oracle and reflection circuits § How is a phase oracle built? To mark one bit string, flip with \\(X\\) each of its positions that is \\(0\\), apply a phase flip controlled on all qubits being \\(1\\), and undo the flips. For a predicate computed by a circuit, use compute, phase, uncompute. And the reflection \\(D\\)? Since \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) maps \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) to \\(\\ket s\\), we can reflect about \\(\\ket{0^n}\\) between two layers of Hadamards: \\[D=H^{\\otimes n}\\bigl(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I\\bigr)H^{\\otimes n}.\\] The two-qubit drawing implements the reflection about \\(\\ket{00}\\) with \\(X\\) gates and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and so produces \\(-D\\). The sign is a global phase and changes nothing in an ordinary Grover iteration ; it would matter if the iteration were itself controlled. The oracle and reflection are decomposed here for one explicit predicate; a general predicate needs its own circuit. Problems Which gates mark the string \\(01\\) on two qubits, that is, multiply \\(\\ket{01}\\) by \\(-1\\) and leave the other basis states alone? For two qubits, verify that \\((H\\otimes H)(2\\ket{00}\\bra{00}-I)(H\\otimes H)\\) and \\(2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\) agree on \\(\\ket{00}\\). Show that \\((X\\otimes X)\\,CZ\\,(X\\otimes X)=-(2\\ket{00}\\bra{00}-I)\\). Why does this sign not matter in an ordinary Grover iteration, and when would it matter?"}, {"title": "Limits of local operations", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Limits of local operations § Can one-qubit gates alone produce every operation? A circuit of one-qubit gates acts as \\(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\). On a product state it gives another product state: \\[(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n)(\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\ket{\\psi_n})=U_1\\ket{\\psi_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\ket{\\psi_n}.\\] It can never prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\), however many gates it uses. The argument settles the question without counting anything: a universal set needs a gate that lets qubits affect one another. Applying \\(A\\) to one qubit and \\(B\\) to the other gives \\((A\\ket\\psi)\\otimes(B\\ket\\phi)\\). Starting with a product, local unitary gates alone cannot create entanglement. Problems Apply \\(H\\otimes H\\) to \\(\\ket{00}\\), and check that the result is a product state. A circuit applies \\(H\\) to qubit 1, then \\(X\\) to qubit 2, then \\(H\\) to qubit 1 again. Find the result on \\(\\ket{00}\\), and explain why it had to be a product state. Show that one-qubit operations cannot turn an entangled two-qubit state into a product state."}, {"title": "Entangling gates and universality", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Entangling gates and universality § Is a single entangling gate enough? It is. One-qubit gates together with CNOT can build every unitary on any number of qubits; a set with this property is universal . One proof writes a unitary as a product of operations that each act on just two basis states, and builds each of these from controlled operations and one-qubit rotations. We state the theorem without the full proof. Does universality make every operation cheap? No. A general unitary on \\(n\\) qubits has a number of parameters exponential in \\(n\\), and most such unitaries need exponentially many gates. Algorithms are useful because their operations have structure that permits short circuits. Further detail: Barenco et al.: Elementary gates for quantum computation . Arbitrary one-qubit unitaries together with CNOT suffice to construct arbitrary finite-register unitaries. A chosen finite gate alphabet instead generally requires approximation. Problems Which of these gate sets can prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\): \\(\\{H,X,Z\\}\\); \\(\\{H,\\text{CNOT}\\}\\); \\(\\{\\text{CNOT},X\\}\\)? Show that circuits of CNOT and \\(X\\) gates only permute computational basis states. A reversible circuit on \\(m\\) wires uses \\(g\\) gates, each an \\(X\\), CNOT, or Toffoli on chosen wires. Show that there are at most \\((3m^3)^g\\) such circuits, and deduce that most Boolean functions of \\(n\\) bits need at least about \\(2^n/\\log_2(3m^3)\\) gates."}, {"title": "Exact synthesis and approximation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Exact synthesis and approximation § Real hardware offers a finite set of gates. Can a finite set build every unitary exactly? No: there are only countably many finite circuits, but uncountably many unitaries. The best a finite set can do is approximate every unitary as closely as we wish. The Clifford+T set, built from \\(H\\), \\(S\\), CNOT, and \\(T\\), does this. How do we measure “close”? Use the operator-norm error \\(\\|U-V\\|\\leq\\epsilon\\): for every unit input, the two outputs differ by a vector of length at most \\(\\epsilon\\). What happens in a circuit of \\(L\\) approximated gates? The errors add at most: if gate \\(i\\) is within \\(\\epsilon_i\\), the whole circuit is within \\(\\sum_i\\epsilon_i\\). A total tolerance \\(\\epsilon\\) can therefore be met by allowing each gate \\(\\epsilon/L\\). A gate count means little without the accuracy it was computed for. The task is to find a finite circuit \\(V\\) close enough to a target \\(U\\). The tolerance controls acceptable error and affects the required circuit length. Problems Each of \\(1000\\) gates is approximated within \\(10^{-6}\\). What error bound holds for the whole circuit? A circuit has \\(10^6\\) gates and its total error must be at most \\(10^{-2}\\). What accuracy must each gate have if the error is split equally? Prove that \\(\\|U_2U_1-V_2V_1\\|\\leq\\|U_2-V_2\\|+\\|U_1-V_1\\|\\) for unitaries \\(U_1,U_2,V_1,V_2\\)."}, {"title": "Gate count, depth, and workspace", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-1", "text": "8.4.1 Gate count, depth, and workspace § What does a circuit cost? Three measures matter: the gate count , the total number of elementary gates; the depth , the number of layers of gates that must run one after another; and the width , the number of qubits, including extra workspace qubits called ancillas . These trade off. More workspace can reduce depth, while recomputing an intermediate value instead of storing it can save qubits. Ancillas must start in a known state and are usually returned to it by uncomputation , so that they can be reused. There is a subtler cost. An algorithm needs a circuit for every input length \\(n\\). A circuit family is uniform if a classical procedure produces the circuit for each \\(n\\), and the family counts as efficient only if that procedure is efficient too. Otherwise a short circuit could hide hard work in its design. Four independent gates on four wires can occupy one layer. Four dependent gates on one wire require four layers. Parallelism may use more workspace. Problems A circuit on three qubits applies \\(H\\) to each qubit, then CNOT from qubit 1 to qubit 2, then CNOT from qubit 2 to qubit 3. Give its gate count, depth, and width. Compute the AND of \\(n=8\\) bits with a chain of Toffoli gates: \\(w_1=x_1x_2\\), \\(w_2=w_1x_3\\), and so on, then the output, then uncompute the workspace. Give the gate count, depth, and width. Compute the AND of the same \\(8\\) bits with a balanced tree instead: combine bits in pairs, then pairs of results, and so on. Compare gate count, depth, and workspace with the chain."}, {"title": "Connectivity and data preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-2", "text": "8.4.2 Connectivity and data preparation § Can any two qubits interact? In a circuit diagram, yes; in a device, often only neighbouring qubits can. Gates between distant qubits then need SWAP gates to bring them together, which adds to the count and the depth. Is loading data free? Preparing an arbitrary list of amplitudes can take work proportional to the length of the list, even though the register holding it is small. Likewise, the arithmetic that implements a predicate can cost far more than the number of oracle calls suggests. A resource estimate must therefore state the gate set, the connectivity, how inputs are prepared, and how outputs are read, before an asymptotic advantage can become a claim about real machines. On a three-site line only neighboring sites interact directly. To act on logical qubits A and C, swap A next to C, apply the gate, and restore the placement if needed. Problems Three qubits sit on a line, \\(1\\)–\\(2\\)–\\(3\\), and only neighbours can interact. How many SWAP gates are needed to apply a CNOT between qubits 1 and 3 and restore the original placement? A SWAP gate costs three CNOT gates. Count the CNOT gates in the construction above, and for a CNOT between the two ends of a line of \\(n\\) qubits. A data set of \\(N=2^n\\) numbers is loaded into amplitudes using one gate per number, and then searched with about \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries. When does the quadratic query speedup survive in the total cost?"}, {"title": "Error accumulation and reliability", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-3", "text": "8.4.3 Error accumulation and reliability § The costs so far assume that every gate does exactly what it should. Real gates are imperfect. Small systematic errors and random noise accumulate over a long circuit. This physical noise differs from the approximation error of gate synthesis, although both change the predictions. Can errors be corrected? Quantum error correction encodes one logical qubit in correlations among several physical qubits, measures error syndromes that reveal which error occurred without revealing the encoded state, and then corrects the error. Does this contradict the no-cloning theorem? No: a code does not make independent copies of the state; it spreads the state into correlations. This book does not develop noise models or the threshold theorem. Reliability is a further resource requirement, beyond proving that an ideal algorithm is correct. For ideal and implemented unitary gates with operator-norm error at most ε per gate, a telescoping bound gives total error at most mε. The bound is not a claim that all errors add with the same sign in a particular run. Problems Each gate fails independently with probability \\(10^{-3}\\). What is the probability that a circuit of \\(1000\\) gates runs without any failure? How small must the failure probability per gate be for a circuit of \\(10^9\\) gates to run without failure with probability at least \\(0.9\\)? A classical repetition code stores a bit \\(b\\) as \\(bbb\\) and decodes by majority. If each copy flips independently with probability \\(q\\), show that decoding fails with probability \\(3q^2-2q^3\\), and that this is smaller than \\(q\\) exactly when \\(q\\lt 1/2\\)."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-1", "text": "Do \\(H\\) followed by \\(Z\\), and \\(Z\\) followed by \\(H\\), have the same action on \\(\\ket0\\)? No. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\); \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket+\\). The order of the matrices must follow the order in time."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-2", "text": "Build the complete two-qubit Grover circuit for the marked item \\(10\\): the oracle from an \\(X\\) gate and a controlled-\\(Z\\), and the reflection from \\(H\\), \\(X\\), and controlled-\\(Z\\) gates. Verify that one iteration from \\(\\ket s\\) gives \\(10\\). Oracle: \\(X\\) on qubit 2, controlled-\\(Z\\), \\(X\\) on qubit 2. It sends \\(\\ket{10}\\) to \\(\\ket{11}\\), applies the sign, and restores \\(\\ket{10}\\). Starting from \\(\\ket s=\\tfrac12(1,1,1,1)\\), the oracle gives \\(\\tfrac12(1,1,-1,1)\\). The reflection circuit implements \\(-D\\), and \\(D\\) maps this state to \\(\\ket{10}\\), so the result is \\(-\\ket{10}\\): the outcome is \\(10\\) with certainty."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-3", "text": "A search over \\(N=2^n\\) items uses \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) iterations. The oracle is compiled into \\(20n\\) gates and the reflection into \\(10n\\) gates. Give the total gate count as a function of \\(n\\), and evaluate it for \\(n=30\\). The total is about \\((\\pi/4)\\,2^{n/2}\\cdot30n\\). For \\(n=30\\): \\((\\pi/4)\\times32{,}768\\times900\\approx2.3\\times10^7\\) gates. The query count, about \\(2.6\\times10^4\\), hides a factor of \\(30n=900\\) gates per query."}, {"title": "Input size and success criteria", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 Input size and success criteria § When can we say that one method is faster than another? Only when both solve the same task on the same terms: the same encoding of inputs, the same measure of input size, the same access to data, and the same standard of success. Why does the encoding matter? Size is measured by the length of the input. An integer \\(N\\) written in binary has about \\(\\log_2N\\) bits, so a procedure that takes \\(N\\) steps takes a number of steps exponential in the input length. Which cost do we report? The worst case over all inputs, the average over a stated distribution of inputs, or the expected cost of an algorithm that uses randomness. Each is legitimate, but they must not be mixed. In a query model, we must also say what one query returns. Changing the interface changes the problem, not merely the method used to solve it. Binary encoding uses logarithmically many bits. A cost proportional to the integer’s value can therefore be exponential in the number of input bits. Problems How many bits does \\(N=10^9\\) have in binary? A procedure takes about \\(\\sqrt N\\) steps. Is that polynomial in the input length? An algorithm takes \\(5\\) steps on half of the \\(n\\)-bit inputs and \\(2^n\\) steps on the other half. Give its worst-case cost and its average cost over uniformly random inputs. Trial division finds a factor of \\(N\\) by testing the divisors \\(2,3,\\dots,\\lfloor\\sqrt N\\rfloor\\). Express its worst-case number of divisions in terms of the input length \\(n=\\log_2N\\). Is it polynomial-time?"}, {"title": "Asymptotic notation", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 Asymptotic notation § Costs are functions of the input length \\(n\\). How do we compare their growth without tracking every constant? We write \\(T(n)=O(g(n))\\) if there is a constant \\(C\\gt 0\\) with \\(T(n)\\leq Cg(n)\\) for all large enough \\(n\\): an eventual upper bound. \\(\\Omega(g(n))\\) is the corresponding lower bound , and \\(\\Theta(g(n))\\) means both. For example, \\(3n^2+7n+2=\\Theta(n^2)\\): for \\(n\\geq1\\) it lies between \\(n^2\\) and \\(12n^2\\). What do these notations hide? Constant factors, and nothing else: not the encoding, and not the model. A polynomial bound \\(n^k\\) grows far more slowly than an exponential bound such as \\(2^n\\). Still, a polynomial with a large exponent can be impractical at the sizes that matter. The linear algorithm with a large constant eventually wins, but not for every input size. Big-O notation describes growth, not a complete timing prediction. Problems Is \\(100n=O(n^2)\\)? Is \\(n^2=O(100n)\\)? Give constants, or explain why none exist. Order these functions by growth: \\(n\\log n\\), \\(2^n\\), \\(n^{10}\\), \\(n^2\\), \\(1.01^n\\), \\(\\sqrt n\\). For which input lengths \\(n\\geq2\\) is an algorithm costing \\(n^{10}\\) steps faster than one costing \\(2^n\\) steps? What does this show about asymptotic comparisons?"}, {"title": "Query, gate, and space complexity", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 Query, gate, and space complexity § Which resource are we counting? Query complexity counts oracle uses, gate complexity counts elementary operations, and space complexity counts memory. Physical running time depends further on scheduling, error correction, and the device. Does an advantage in one resource carry over to another? Not automatically. A quantum query advantage may survive, or disappear, once the cost of implementing each query and of preparing the input is counted. For unstructured search, the quadratic reduction in queries is a theorem. It is not a theorem that searching any stored dataset on any quantum device is quadratically faster overall. These totals count only the gates inside query calls. Preparation and other gates must still be added for a full comparison. Problems A Grover search over \\(N=10^6\\) items uses about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\) queries. If each query costs \\(1000\\) gates and each reflection \\(50\\) gates, estimate the total gate count. A classical scan costs one operation per item. For \\(N=10^6\\), below which cost \\(c\\) per query does a Grover search, costing about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\cdot c\\) gates, beat the scan? Algorithm A uses \\(n^2\\) queries and \\(n^3\\) other gates; algorithm B uses \\(n\\) queries and \\(n^4\\) other gates. If each query costs \\(n^k\\) gates, which algorithm has the smaller total gate count, depending on \\(k\\)?"}, {"title": "P, NP, and reductions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 P, NP, and reductions § Which problems count as efficiently solvable? Consider decision problems , whose answer is yes or no. P is the class of decision problems that a deterministic algorithm solves in time polynomial in the input length. Some problems seem harder to solve than to check. NP is the class of decision problems whose yes instances have a witness , a certificate of polynomial size that can be checked in polynomial time. Equivalently, a nondeterministic machine accepts when some possible run guesses a witness and checks it. This is not a claim that a random guess will probably succeed. How do we compare the difficulty of two problems? A polynomial-time reduction from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) converts each instance of \\(A\\) into an instance of \\(B\\) with the same answer, so a fast solver for \\(B\\) gives a fast solver for \\(A\\). Whether P equals NP, that is, whether every efficiently checkable problem is efficiently solvable, is unknown. If task \\(A\\) reduces efficiently to task \\(B\\), an efficient solver for \\(B\\) would yield one for \\(A\\). The arrow describes converting instances, not proving that such a solver exists. Problems Which of these are decision problems: (a) is \\(N\\) prime? (b) find a factor of \\(N\\); (c) does a graph \\(G\\) contain a path from \\(u\\) to \\(v\\)? For the problem “does \\(N\\) have a factor \\(d\\) with \\(1\\lt d\\lt k\\)?”, give a witness for \\(N=91\\) and \\(k=10\\), and explain how a witness is checked in polynomial time. Prove that if \\(A\\) reduces to \\(B\\) in polynomial time and \\(B\\) is in P, then \\(A\\) is in P. Why must the reduction itself run in polynomial time?"}, {"title": "BPP and BQP", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 BPP and BQP § What if an algorithm may use randomness and occasionally err? BPP is the class of decision problems solved in polynomial time by a randomized classical algorithm whose probability of error is at most \\(1/3\\) on every input. Why \\(1/3\\)? Any constant below \\(1/2\\) would do: repeating the algorithm and taking the majority answer drives the error down quickly. BQP is the quantum counterpart: decision problems solved, with error at most \\(1/3\\), by a uniform family of polynomial-size quantum circuits. How do the classes compare? A quantum circuit can simulate a randomized classical computation, so \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\). Is BQP larger? This is not known, and neither is whether BQP contains NP. These are classes of decision problems; factoring is usually stated as a task whose answer is a number. Both classes concern polynomial-time decision procedures with a bounded error on every allowed input. The computational model differs. Problems An algorithm errs with probability at most \\(1/3\\). Run it three times independently and take the majority answer. What is the error probability? How many independent runs, with a majority vote, bring the error below \\(0.01\\) when each run errs with probability \\(1/3\\)? Show that an algorithm that ignores its input and answers by flipping a fair coin has error \\(1/2\\) on every input, and that majority voting over repeated runs never improves it. Why is the condition “error at most \\(1/3\\)” essential?"}, {"title": "Factoring and search", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Factoring and search § What evidence is there that quantum computers are more powerful? There are two kinds, of different strength. Factoring: quantum period finding gives a factoring algorithm whose running time is polynomial in the number of digits; its derivation lies beyond this book. No polynomial-time classical algorithm is known. No one has proved that none exists, however, so this is an advantage over known methods, not a proven separation. Search: Grover’s algorithm has a proved quadratic advantage, but only in the unstructured query model. Neither kind of evidence suggests that quantum computers speed up every task. Factoring uses arithmetic structure; unstructured search assumes only oracle access to a predicate. A lower bound for the latter is not a lower bound for every structured problem. Problems Trial division of a \\(2048\\)-bit number may test up to \\(\\sqrt N\\approx2^{1024}\\) divisors. Write this roughly as a power of ten. A rough estimate for Shor’s factoring algorithm is \\(n^3\\) operations for an \\(n\\)-bit number. Compare \\(n^3\\) with \\(2^{n/2}\\) for \\(n=2048\\). The best known classical factoring method, the number field sieve, takes time roughly \\(\\exp\\bigl(1.92(\\ln N)^{1/3}(\\ln\\ln N)^{2/3}\\bigr)\\). Evaluate it for a \\(2048\\)-bit \\(N\\), and explain why this comparison does not prove that quantum computers are exponentially more powerful."}, {"title": "Upper and lower bounds", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Upper and lower bounds § How do we know a problem cannot be solved faster? An upper bound needs only one algorithm and an analysis of its cost. A lower bound must hold for every algorithm the model allows, including ones that nobody has invented. A slow known method proves nothing about faster unknown ones. Why are query models useful here? In a query model, an algorithm may do anything it likes between queries, but the hidden input affects it only through the queries it makes. That restriction is what makes lower-bound arguments possible. A query lower bound is strong within the model, though it says nothing by itself about the cost of each query. A construction proves that a task can be solved within a cost. A lower bound must cover every permitted algorithm in the stated model. Problems Which of these establishes a lower bound on query complexity: (a) an algorithm using \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries; (b) a proof that every algorithm needs at least \\(\\sqrt N\\) queries; (c) many failed attempts to find an algorithm with fewer queries? Using the fact that \\(k\\) classical queries find a single marked item among \\(N\\) with probability at most \\(k/N\\), show that success probability \\(1/2\\) needs at least \\(N/2\\) queries. In the query model, an algorithm may compute without limit between queries. Explain why a query lower bound still applies to algorithms with limited computation, but a query upper bound need not give a fast algorithm."}, {"title": "Unstructured search optimality", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Unstructured search optimality § Can a quantum algorithm search faster than Grover’s? No. Finding a unique marked item among \\(N\\) with constant success probability requires \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries. Grover’s algorithm uses \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\), so the query complexity of the task is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). Why can it not be faster? A single query can change the state substantially only through the amplitude on the marked label, and averaged over all possible positions of the mark, that amplitude is small. The hybrid argument makes this precise; we state its conclusion as a theorem. The geometry of the Grover iteration proves an upper bound, not optimality. For one marked item and constant success in the unstructured quantum query model, Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) upper bound and the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound give \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) query complexity. Problems For \\(N=10^6\\), compare Grover’s query count, about \\((\\pi/4)\\sqrt N\\), with the classical average \\(N/2\\). If some quantum algorithm searched with only \\(N^{1/3}\\) queries, how many would it use for \\(N=10^6\\)? Which result rules this out? Use the lower bound to show that no quantum algorithm finds a marked item among \\(N=2^n\\) with a number of queries polynomial in \\(n\\). What does this imply for solving NP problems by unstructured search?"}, {"title": "Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional)", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional) § Optional derivation · polynomial lower bounds Is there another route to lower bounds? Write the hidden input of the oracle as bits \\(z_1,\\dots,z_N\\). After \\(T\\) queries, every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in these bits, and every acceptance probability is a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\). An algorithm that is correct with probability at least \\(2/3\\) therefore yields a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\) that approximates the function it computes on every Boolean input. If every such approximating polynomial needs degree at least \\(d\\), the approximate degree , then \\(2T\\geq d\\), so \\(T\\geq d/2\\). For OR on \\(N\\) bits, which detects whether any item is marked, the approximate degree is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). This gives a second proof of the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound. Appendix E explains why the degree grows by at most one per query; the approximate degree of OR is a separate theorem. Further detail: Beals et al.: Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials . The query model limits acceptance degree to \\(2T\\). If approximating the requested function requires degree \\(d\\), then \\(2T\\ge d\\). The approximate-degree theorem is a separate ingredient. Problems After \\(T=3\\) queries, what is the largest possible degree of an amplitude, and of an acceptance probability? The approximate degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits grows like \\(\\sqrt N\\). For \\(N=10^4\\), what lower bound on queries does \\(T\\geq d/2\\) give, up to a constant factor? Show that the exact degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits is \\(N\\), by writing \\(\\mathrm{OR}(z)=1-\\prod_i(1-z_i)\\). What does this imply for algorithms that must be correct with certainty?"}, {"title": "Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-1", "text": "9.4.1 Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions § What do the algorithms of this book have in common? Each uses coherent operations to make the property we want affect the probabilities of the final outcome. Deutsch’s algorithm turns the equality of two function values into a relative phase , and then into a definite bit. Amplitude amplification rotates a prepared state toward the successful outcomes. What do their advantages depend on? Specified oracles, coherent access to inverses where they are used, and a measurement chosen to expose the answer. The large state space gives room for these constructions, but size alone explains nothing: correctness is established by calculating the evolution and the readout. An advantage claim must include the access assumptions and success criterion. Expensive preparation or readout can dominate a reduction in oracle calls. Problems For Deutsch’s algorithm and for Grover search, name the property that is encoded in phases, and the operation that turns it into probability. For each of the two algorithms, list costs that its query count does not include. An algorithm prepares the uniform superposition of \\(n\\) qubits, applies an oracle once, and measures immediately. Show that it does no better than one classical query, whether the oracle is a phase oracle or the reversible \\(U_f\\)."}, {"title": "Computability and readout limitations", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-2", "text": "9.4.2 Computability and readout limitations § What can quantum computers not do? They do not escape the halting problem : a classical computer can simulate a quantum one, slowly, so both compute the same functions. They cannot copy unknown states, and a single measurement does not reveal all the amplitudes. Nor do they remove the cost of encoding inputs, building operations, or protecting states from noise. These constraints are part of the model, not footnotes added afterward. A useful claim of advantage names the task, the mechanism, the success guarantee, and the resources counted. The care that separates a pattern from a proof in mathematics also separates a plausible speedup from an established one. A faster implementation does not turn an undecidable task into a decidable one, nor make all amplitudes available from a single measurement. Problems Can a quantum computer decide the halting problem? Why not? A classical simulation of an \\(n\\)-qubit circuit stores all \\(2^n\\) amplitudes. How does its memory grow, and why does the simulation nevertheless show that quantum computers compute nothing beyond Turing machines? Each measurement of an \\(n\\)-qubit state yields \\(n\\) bits. Show that learning a general state’s \\(2^n\\) amplitudes to useful accuracy needs a number of copies exponential in \\(n\\)."}, {"title": "Period finding and error correction: further topics", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-3", "text": "9.4.3 Period finding and error correction: further topics § Where does the subject go next? Period finding uses interference in a new way: a quantum Fourier transform turns periodic structure in a function into frequencies that a measurement reveals. Quantum error correction protects long computations by encoding states in subspaces and measuring syndromes. Both build on the language developed here, of states, operators, measurements, and circuits, but they need further constructions and proofs. They continue the present algorithms rather than underlie them. Whatever the algorithm, the same three things must be shown: a mechanism, a correct readout, and an honest resource bound. Period finding concerns making structure observable; error correction concerns protecting a computation from faults. A useful large computation needs both suitable algorithms and sufficiently reliable operations. Problems What is the period of \\(f(x)=2^x\\bmod15\\)? Use the period \\(r=4\\) of \\(2^x\\bmod15\\) to factor \\(15\\), by computing \\(\\gcd(2^{r/2}-1,15)\\) and \\(\\gcd(2^{r/2}+1,15)\\). Let \\(a^x\\bmod N\\) have even period \\(r\\), with \\(a^{r/2}\\not\\equiv-1\\pmod N\\). Show that \\(\\gcd(a^{r/2}-1,N)\\) is a factor of \\(N\\) other than \\(1\\) and \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-1", "text": "An algorithm uses \\(O(N)\\) steps on an integer \\(N\\) written in binary. Is that polynomial in the input length? Not in general. The input length is about \\(\\log_2N\\), so \\(N\\) steps can be exponential in that length."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-2", "text": "One algorithm uses at most \\(3n^2+7n+2\\) operations, and at least \\(n^2\\). Give constants showing that its cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). Does this prove that every algorithm for the task needs quadratic time? For \\(n\\geq1\\), \\(n^2\\leq3n^2+7n+2\\leq3n^2+7n^2+2n^2=12n^2\\), so the cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\) with constants \\(1\\) and \\(12\\). This describes one algorithm. A lower bound for the task would have to hold for every algorithm, which needs a separate argument."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-3", "text": "A quantum search uses \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) queries, but each query requires \\(\\Theta(N)\\) gates in a chosen implementation. What do the query and gate counts say about this implementation, and about the task? The queries alone contribute \\(\\Theta(N^{3/2})\\) gates, worse than a classical scan of \\(N\\) items. The quadratic query advantage is still a true statement about oracle access. It does not show that this implementation beats a classical method, and a cheaper oracle implementation could change the comparison."}, {"title": "Automata and Memory", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-a", "text": "A. Automata and Memory How much memory does a computation need? Start with the least: a finite automaton , which reads its input once while keeping one of finitely many control states. Two states suffice to track parity, whether the number of ones read so far is even or odd: reading a one swaps the states, and reading a zero leaves them unchanged. Can a finite automaton check that a string consists of some number of \\(a\\)’s followed by the same number of \\(b\\)’s, the language \\(\\{a^nb^n:n\\geq0\\}\\)? No. A fixed automaton has finitely many states, so two different long prefixes \\(a^i\\) and \\(a^j\\) must reach the same state. Appending \\(b^i\\) then forces the same decision on \\(a^ib^i\\), which is in the language, and on \\(a^jb^i\\), which is not. This state-collision argument proves the limitation. What extra memory is enough? A stack , which can hold arbitrarily many pending symbols: push once for each \\(a\\), pop once for each \\(b\\), and accept when the input and the stack run out together. A pushdown automaton formalizes this memory. A linear-bounded automaton has a writable tape no longer than a constant multiple of the input, and a Turing machine removes even that bound. Do these models form a sequence of ever better machines? Not in that sense. More memory changes which configurations are available, not what a single step means. The models describe different classes of languages; they are not successive generations of hardware. Reading \\(aabb\\) pushes for each \\(a\\) and pops for each \\(b\\). The empty stack at the end certifies matching counts in this example; checking order is also required. Problems Design a two-state finite automaton that accepts the binary strings with an even number of \\(1\\)s, and trace it on \\(1011\\). Run the stack procedure on \\(aaabbb\\) and on \\(aabbb\\), giving the stack height after each symbol. Show that no finite automaton recognizes the strings over \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) with equal numbers of \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s, in any order."}, {"title": "Grammars and Lambda Calculus", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-b", "text": "B. Grammars and Lambda Calculus Instead of recognizing strings, can we describe a set of strings by how its members are produced? A grammar does this. It specifies a start symbol, terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, and replacement rules. The rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), where \\(\\epsilon\\) is the empty string, generate exactly the strings of matching \\(a\\)’s and \\(b\\)’s: applying the first rule twice and then the second gives \\(aabb\\). How do grammars relate to machines? Generation asks what can be produced; recognition asks whether a given string belongs to the generated set. Regular grammars correspond to finite automata, context-free grammars to pushdown automata, context-sensitive grammars to linear-bounded automata, and unrestricted grammars to the languages a Turing machine recognizes, with the usual conventions about the empty string. Can computation be described without any machine at all? Lambda calculus uses only functions, application, and substitution. The reduction \\((\\lambda x.t)u\\to t[x:=u]\\) replaces the free occurrences of \\(x\\) in \\(t\\) by \\(u\\), renaming bound variables where necessary to avoid capture; for example, \\((\\lambda x.x)u\\to u\\). How can functions alone represent numbers? A Church numeral \\(n\\) is the function that applies a given function \\(n\\) times to a given starting value, so three is \\(\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f(f(x)))\\). Functions alone can encode data and iteration. The functions computable in lambda calculus are exactly the Turing-computable ones. That equivalence between formal models supports the broader Church–Turing thesis; it does not prove it. Apply \\(S\\to aSb\\) twice, then \\(S\\to\\) empty string. This demonstrates generation of \\(aabb\\); it does not yet give a procedure for rejecting arbitrary strings. Problems Derive \\(aaabbb\\) from \\(S\\) with the rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\), writing each step. Reduce \\((\\lambda x.\\lambda y.x)\\,a\\,b\\) step by step. With Church numerals, show that \\(\\mathrm{SUCC}=\\lambda n.\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(n\\,f\\,x)\\) applied to \\(2=\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f\\,x)\\) gives \\(3\\)."}, {"title": "Linear Algebra Reference", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-c", "text": "C. Linear Algebra Reference This appendix collects the linear algebra used in the book; each identity is explained where it is first needed. How are complex vectors compared? Through the inner product \\(\\braket uv=u^\\dagger v\\), where \\(u^\\dagger=(u^*)^T\\) is the conjugate transpose. The norm is \\(\\sqrt{u^\\dagger u}\\). An orthonormal basis matrix \\(B\\) satisfies \\(B^\\dagger B=I\\), and \\(B^\\dagger\\) converts old coordinates to new ones. In which order do matrices act? From right to left on column vectors, and the adjoint reverses the order of a product: \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). Three properties of a matrix are easily confused. A unitary preserves overlaps; a Hermitian matrix equals its adjoint; an orthogonal projector satisfies \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\). How do tensor products behave? \\((A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=Au\\otimes Bv\\) and \\(\\braket{u\\otimes v}{x\\otimes y}=\\braket ux\\braket vy\\). In the binary ordering used in this book, the leftmost qubit is the most significant label. How large can a matrix make a vector? The operator norm \\(\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Av\\|\\) answers this. It gives uniform error bounds, because \\(\\|(U-V)v\\|\\leq\\|U-V\\|\\) for every unit vector \\(v\\). For the complex vector \\((3,4i)\\), the squared norm is \\(9+16=25\\). Dividing by five gives a unit vector; the imaginary coordinate contributes positively to its norm. Problems What is the operator norm of \\(\\operatorname{diag}(3,1)\\), and of a unitary \\(U\\)? Verify \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\) for \\(A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(B=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). Show that a projector \\(P\\), with \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\), has only the eigenvalues \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and that \\(I-P\\) is also a projector."}, {"title": "Mixed States and General Measurements", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-d", "text": "D. Mixed States and General Measurements What if we know only that a system was prepared in one of several states, \\(\\ket{\\psi_r}\\) with probability \\(q_r\\)? No single state vector describes it. The density matrix \\[\\rho=\\sum_rq_r\\ket{\\psi_r}\\bra{\\psi_r}\\] does. It is positive semidefinite and has trace one, and a pure state has \\(\\rho=\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi\\). Different ensembles can give the same density matrix, and then they give the same predictions for every measurement. The measurement rule becomes \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\), and unitary evolution sends \\(\\rho\\) to \\(U\\rho U^\\dagger\\). What is the state of one part of an entangled pair? Its reduced state \\(\\rho_A=\\operatorname{tr}_B\\rho_{AB}\\), obtained by summing the diagonal blocks for \\(B\\) in a product basis. For either half of a Bell state , it is \\(I/2\\). Can an operation on \\(B\\) change \\(\\rho_A\\)? Not when its outcome is ignored. A trace-preserving operation on \\(B\\), with operators \\(M_r\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_rM_r^\\dagger M_r=I\\), leaves \\(\\rho_A\\) unchanged; inserting the sum into the partial trace shows this. Conditioning on the outcome \\(r\\) changes the description only for someone who knows \\(r\\). Are projective measurements the only ones? No. A general measurement has positive operators \\(E_j\\), called effects , that sum to the identity, with probabilities \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\). The effects fix the probabilities but not the state after the measurement; measurement operators supply that further information. What can a general measurement do that a projective one cannot? Suppose the state is either \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\). With \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), take \\[E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-,\\qquad E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1,\\qquad E_?=I-E_0-E_+.\\] The outcome \\(0\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket+\\), the outcome \\(+\\) is impossible for \\(\\ket0\\), and the outcome \\(?\\) is inconclusive. \\(E_?\\) is positive because the largest eigenvalue of \\(\\ket-\\bra-+\\ket1\\bra1\\) is \\(1+1/\\sqrt2\\). Whenever this measurement gives an answer, the answer is certain; the price is that it sometimes declines to answer. An equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) and an equal mixture of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) both yield \\(I/2\\). No measurement on the qubit alone identifies which ensemble was used. Problems Write the density matrix of the ensemble that prepares \\(\\ket0\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) with probability \\(1/4\\). What is the probability of outcome \\(0\\)? Show that the ensembles \\(\\{\\ket0,\\ket1\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\ket+,\\ket-\\}\\), each with equal probabilities, have the same density matrix. For the measurement with effects \\(E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-\\), \\(E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1\\), and \\(E_?=I-E_0-E_+\\), where \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\), compute the probability of the inconclusive outcome for \\(\\ket0\\) and for \\(\\ket+\\)."}, {"title": "Polynomial Lower Bounds", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-e", "text": "E. Polynomial Lower Bounds Why must a search algorithm make many queries? In the Boolean query model, the hidden input is a string \\(z\\in\\{0,1\\}^N\\), and a query transforms \\(\\ket{i,b}\\) into \\(\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\). How does each query change the amplitudes? Every new amplitude is a combination of previous amplitudes multiplied by \\(z_i\\) or by \\(1-z_i\\). The amplitudes therefore start as constants and gain degree at most one per query. Operations that do not depend on the input only form linear combinations, which do not raise the degree. By induction, after \\(T\\) queries every amplitude is a polynomial of degree at most \\(T\\) in the input bits. What about the probability of accepting? It is a sum of squared magnitudes of amplitudes, so its degree is at most \\(2T\\). On Boolean inputs, any power \\(z_i^k\\) with \\(k\\geq1\\) equals \\(z_i\\), so the polynomial can be taken to be multilinear. The approximate degree of a Boolean function is the least degree of a real polynomial that approximates it, within a fixed error below one half, on every Boolean input. An algorithm with bounded error yields such a polynomial of degree at most \\(2T\\), so its number of queries is at least half the approximate degree. Proving that OR has approximate degree of order \\(\\sqrt N\\) needs an argument from approximation theory, beyond this short derivation. Together with the polynomial representation, that theorem gives the lower bound for detecting a marked item. Neither follows from the observation that known algorithms are slow. Starting from amplitudes independent of hidden input bits, each Boolean query raises their degree by at most one. Squaring magnitudes gives the factor of two for acceptance probabilities. Problems With two hidden bits and the query \\(\\ket{i,b}\\mapsto\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\), start from \\(\\ket{1,0}\\). Write the amplitudes after one query as polynomials in \\(z_1,z_2\\). Use \\(z_i^2=z_i\\) on Boolean inputs to reduce \\(z_1^2z_2+z_1z_2^2\\) to a multilinear polynomial. Write the parity of two bits, \\(z_1\\oplus z_2\\), as a real polynomial, and show that its degree is \\(2\\). What lower bound on queries does this give for an exact algorithm, and is it achieved?"}, {"title": "Adjoint", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adjoint", "text": "The conjugate transpose of a matrix, written with a dagger. It is not generally the inverse."}, {"title": "Algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#algorithm", "text": "A finite set of precise instructions for carrying out a task. Whether it terminates on every allowed input must be established."}, {"title": "Amplitude", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude", "text": "A complex coefficient used to describe a quantum state or transition. The squared magnitude gives the corresponding probability in the specified measurement basis."}, {"title": "Amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-amplification", "text": "A sequence of reflections that increases the amplitude in a designated successful subspace, given suitable preparation and inverse operations."}, {"title": "Bell state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bell-state", "text": "One of four maximally entangled two-qubit basis states. The book begins with the equal superposition of 00 and 11."}, {"title": "Boolean function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#boolean-function", "text": "A function whose inputs and outputs are bits. A truth table specifies its value for every input."}, {"title": "BQP", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bqp", "text": "The class of decision problems solvable by uniform polynomial-size quantum circuits with bounded error."}, {"title": "Circuit depth", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#circuit-depth", "text": "The number of sequential layers of operations when compatible operations can run in parallel."}, {"title": "Computability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#computability", "text": "Whether a task can be performed by an effective procedure, without imposing an efficiency requirement."}, {"title": "Consistency", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#consistency", "text": "A formal theory is consistent if its rules cannot prove both a statement and its negation."}, {"title": "Decision problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#decision-problem", "text": "A problem whose required answer is yes or no for each allowed input."}, {"title": "Entanglement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#entanglement", "text": "For a pure joint state, the inability to write it as a product of subsystem states. Mixed-state entanglement requires a broader definition."}, {"title": "Global phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#global-phase", "text": "A common phase factor multiplying an entire state vector. It leaves all measurement probabilities unchanged."}, {"title": "Grover iteration", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#grover-iteration", "text": "A phase reversal on marked items followed by a reflection about the prepared uniform state in the standard search algorithm."}, {"title": "Halting problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#halting-problem", "text": "The task of deciding, for every program and input, whether the program eventually stops. No algorithm solves it in full generality."}, {"title": "Hermitian", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hermitian", "text": "An operator equal to its adjoint. Its eigenvalues are real and its eigenspaces can describe a projective measurement."}, {"title": "Inner product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inner-product", "text": "A scalar obtained from two vectors by conjugating the coordinates of the first and summing their products with the second. It measures their overlap."}, {"title": "Interference", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#interference", "text": "The addition of amplitudes for indistinguishable alternatives, which can increase or decrease an outcome probability."}, {"title": "Lower bound", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#lower-bound", "text": "A limit on the resources required by every algorithm within a specified model, rather than the cost of one construction."}, {"title": "NAND", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#nand", "text": "The Boolean operation that returns zero exactly when both input bits are one. NAND gates suffice to build every Boolean function."}, {"title": "Normalization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#normalization", "text": "The requirement that a state vector has squared norm one, so the probabilities of a complete basis measurement sum to one."}, {"title": "Oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oracle", "text": "A specified operation that provides access to a function. Counting its uses does not by itself account for the cost of building it."}, {"title": "Phase kickback", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-kickback", "text": "When a controlled operation acts on a target eigenstate, its eigenvalue appears as a phase on the corresponding control component."}, {"title": "Product state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-state", "text": "A pure joint state that can be written as a tensor product of one state for each subsystem."}, {"title": "Projective measurement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#projective-measurement", "text": "A measurement described by mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity. Each outcome probability is the squared norm of the projected state."}, {"title": "Quantum advantage", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-advantage", "text": "An improvement over classical computation for a specified task, resource measure, and success guarantee. Its assumptions must be stated."}, {"title": "Quantum circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-circuit", "text": "An ordered description of operations on quantum registers, with any measurements and classical control specified."}, {"title": "Qubit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#qubit", "text": "A two-state quantum system. Its state is described by two amplitudes, one for each of the labels zero and one."}, {"title": "Query complexity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-complexity", "text": "The number of oracle calls required for a task under a stated success criterion and oracle model."}, {"title": "Relative phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#relative-phase", "text": "The phase difference between two components of a state. It can affect probabilities after further operations."}, {"title": "Reversible", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reversible", "text": "A transformation is reversible when its output determines its input uniquely on the allowed state space."}, {"title": "Superposition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#superposition", "text": "A linear combination of basis states. Its components depend on the chosen basis; measurement does not reveal all coefficients at once."}, {"title": "Tensor product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#tensor-product", "text": "The construction of a joint vector space from component spaces. Basis labels combine in pairs and dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Transition weight", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#transition-weight", "text": "The number attached to an arrow from one configuration to another: 1 for a deterministic or allowed arrow, a probability for a probabilistic rule, a complex amplitude for a quantum operation."}, {"title": "Turing machine", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#turing-machine", "text": "A mathematical model with a tape, a read/write head, and finitely many control states. A transition rule specifies each step."}, {"title": "Uncomputation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#uncomputation", "text": "Applying the inverse of a computation to clear temporary workspace while retaining the needed result or phase."}, {"title": "Unitary", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#unitary", "text": "An operator whose adjoint is its inverse. Unitary transformations preserve inner products and describe closed-system quantum evolution."}, {"title": "Universal gate set", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#universal-gate-set", "text": "A set of gates sufficient to construct arbitrary quantum operations in the stated model, exactly or to a specified approximation."}, {"title": "Notation: not P", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-not-p", "text": "¬ P The negation of the sentence \\(P\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: f from n-bit strings to bits", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-f-from-n-bit-strings-to-bits", "text": "f:{0,1}ⁿ→{0,1} A Boolean function: one yes/no answer for each \\(n\\)-bit input string."}, {"title": "Notation: a XOR b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-xor-b", "text": "a⊕ b Exclusive OR: one when exactly one input is one. It is addition modulo two."}, {"title": "Notation: a and b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-and-b", "text": "a∧ b AND of two bits: one only when both are one."}, {"title": "Notation: k B T log two", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-k-b-t-log-two", "text": "k_BTln 2 Landauer’s minimum heat for erasing one equally likely bit at temperature \\(T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: x maps to y", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-maps-to-y", "text": "x↦ y The rule that sends each input \\(x\\) to the output \\(y\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: U sub f", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-u-sub-f", "text": "U_f The reversible oracle for \\(f\\): it keeps \\(x\\) and XORs \\(f(x)\\) into a target, \\(U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,y\\oplus f(x)}\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sum over j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sum-over-j", "text": "Σⱼ Add the following term for every value of \\(j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: probability of j given i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-probability-of-j-given-i", "text": "P(j| i) Conditional probability: the chance of next state \\(j\\) when the current state is \\(i\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: r e to the i phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-r-e-to-the-i-phi", "text": "re^(iφ) An amplitude with magnitude \\(r\\ge0\\) and phase \\(\\phi\\). Its outcome probability is \\(r^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: i", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-i", "text": "i The imaginary unit, with \\(i^2=-1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: z star", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-z-star", "text": "z* The complex conjugate: for \\(z=a+ib\\), \\(z^*=a-ib\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: magnitude of z", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-magnitude-of-z", "text": "|z| The size of a complex number. \\(|z|^2=z^*z\\) turns an amplitude into a probability weight."}, {"title": "Notation: v transpose", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-v-transpose", "text": "vᵀ Turns a row into a column or a column into a row; \\((a,b)^T\\) is a column vector."}, {"title": "Notation: ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-psi", "text": "|ψ⟩ The column vector of a state, named \\(\\psi\\). The brackets name a vector; they are not an operation."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero, ket one", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-ket-one", "text": "|0⟩, |1⟩ The computational basis: \\(\\ket0=(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\ket1=(0,1)^T\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi", "text": "⟨φ| The conjugate-transpose row of \\(\\ket\\phi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: bra phi ket psi", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bra-phi-ket-psi", "text": "⟨φ|ψ⟩ The complex inner product, or overlap, \\(\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket plus, ket minus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-plus-ket-minus", "text": "|±⟩ The plus/minus basis, \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: norm of v", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-norm-of-v", "text": "|v| The length of a vector, \\(\\sqrt{\\braket vv}\\). A state vector has norm one."}, {"title": "Notation: ket a tensor ket b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-a-tensor-ket-b", "text": "|a⟩⊗|b⟩ The joint state of two systems, also written \\(\\ket{ab}\\). Dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Notation: Bell state phi plus", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bell-state-phi-plus", "text": "|Φ⁺⟩ The entangled state \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket zero to the n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-zero-to-the-n", "text": "|0ⁿ⟩ All \\(n\\) qubits in state zero."}, {"title": "Notation: identity", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-identity", "text": "I The matrix that leaves every vector unchanged."}, {"title": "Notation: A dagger", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-dagger", "text": "A† The adjoint: the conjugate transpose \\((A^*)^T\\), defined by \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: diagonal a, b", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-diagonal-a-b", "text": "diag(a,b) The diagonal matrix with entries \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: ket e bra e", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-ket-e-bra-e", "text": "|e⟩⟨e| The projector onto the unit vector \\(\\ket e\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: P sub j", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-p-sub-j", "text": "Pⱼ The projector for measurement outcome \\(j\\). The Born rule gives \\(p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda", "text": "λ An eigenvalue: \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: X, Z, H", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-x-z-h", "text": "X, Z, H The bit flip, phase flip, and Hadamard gates."}, {"title": "Notation: A tensor n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-a-tensor-n", "text": "A^(⊗ n) \\(n\\) copies combined by the tensor product. \\(H^{\\otimes n}\\) applies \\(H\\) to each of \\(n\\) qubits; \\(\\ket\\psi^{\\otimes2}\\) is two copies of \\(\\ket\\psi\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: rho", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-rho", "text": "ρ A density matrix, describing a preparation that need not be pure."}, {"title": "Notation: trace", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-trace", "text": "tr The sum of diagonal entries. With \\(\\rho\\), the Born rule is \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: minus one to the f of x", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-minus-one-to-the-f-of-x", "text": "(−1)^(f(x)) The sign a phase oracle attaches to input \\(x\\): \\(+1\\) when \\(f(x)=0\\), \\(-1\\) when \\(f(x)=1\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: sine squared theta equals M over N", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-sine-squared-theta-equals-m-over-n", "text": "sin²θ=M/N Defines the search angle \\(\\theta\\) for \\(M\\) marked items among \\(N\\)."}, {"title": "Notation: big O of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-o-of-g-of-n", "text": "O(g(n)) An eventual upper bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big omega of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-omega-of-g-of-n", "text": "Ω(g(n)) An eventual lower bound up to a constant factor."}, {"title": "Notation: big theta of g of n", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-big-theta-of-g-of-n", "text": "Θ(g(n)) Both an upper and a lower bound: growth exactly like \\(g(n)\\) up to constants."}, {"title": "Notation: BPP is contained in BQP", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-bpp-is-contained-in-bqp", "text": "BPP⊆BQP Every problem in the first class is also in the second."}, {"title": "Notation: lambda x dot t, applied to u", "chapter": "Notation", "url": "notation.html#symbol-lambda-x-dot-t-applied-to-u", "text": "(λ x.t)u In lambda calculus, a function with parameter \\(x\\) and body \\(t\\), applied to \\(u\\)."}];
